CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on the Personnel Management of Civilian Military Employee and matters necessary for the enforcement thereof.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Decree are defined as follows: <Amended on Sep. 5, 2017; Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. The term "appointing authority" means a person who has the authority to appoint civilian military employees in general service (hereinafter referred to as "civilian military employee in general service") under Article 3 of the Act on the Personnel Management of Civilian Military Employee (hereinafter referred to as "the Act") or a person who has been delegated with such authority pursuant to Article 6 of the Act;
  2. The term "person with authority over assignments" means a person who has the authority to assign civilian military employees in general service pursuant to Article 13(1) of the Act or a person who has been delegated with such authority;
  3. The term "general-grade commander of a military unit" means the head of a military unit or agency whose head is, according to the table of organization or table of organization and equipment, a general-grade officer or a civilian military employee in general service equivalent thereto;
  4. The term "commander of a military unit under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense" means the head of a military unit or agency under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 3 (Classification of ranks) (1) The titles of duties by rank for civilian military employees in general service whose ranks are classified pursuant to Article 3(1) of the Act shall be as specified in Appendix 1;
  (2) Deleted. <Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 4 (Criteria for each rank of civilian military employees in general service) The criteria for each rank of civilian military employees in general service and the criteria for comparing the ranks of civilian military employees in general service with those of military personnel when determining persons with authority to take disciplinary actions pursuant to Article 38(1) of the Act shall be as specified in Appendix 3.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 7, 2020]

Article 5 (Personnel records) (1) An appointing authority or a person with authority over assignments shall prepare, maintain, and preserve personnel records of civilian military employees in general service under their authority, and such personnel records may be prepared, maintained, and preserved in electronic form. <Amended on Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) Matters necessary for personnel records shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

CHAPTER II PERSONNEL MANAGEMENT COMMITTEE FOR CIVILIAN MILITARY EMPLOYEE

Article 6 (Functions) (1) The personnel management committee for civilian military employees established in the Ministry of National Defense among the personnel management committees for civilian military employees under Article 5(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Personnel Management Committee") shall deliberate on the following matters: <Amended on Jul. 7, 2020>
  1. Important matters concerning the improvement of the personnel system for civilian military personnel;
  2. Matters concerning ex officio dismissal;
  3. Matters concerning new employment, promotion, and personnel exchange;
  4. Matters concerning the adjustment of performance evaluations;
  5. Other matters referred by the Minister of National Defense for deliberation for fair personnel management.
  (2) The Personnel Management Committee for civilian military employees established in an agency or military unit under Article 5(1)2 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "military unit") shall deliberate on the following matters:
  1. Matters concerning ex officio dismissal;
  2. Matters concerning promotion and personnel exchange;
  3. Matters concerning the adjustment of performance evaluations;
  4. Other matters referred by the commander of a military unit for deliberation for fair personnel management.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 7 (Composition) (1) The Personnel Management Committee shall be comprised of not less than 5 but not more than 7 members, including one chairperson.
  (2) The chairperson of the Personnel Management Committee shall be the member holding the higher position among the members, and the members of the Personnel Management Committee established in the Ministry of National Defense shall be appointed by the Minister of National Defense, and the members of the Personnel Management Committee established in the military unit shall be appointed by the commander of the military unit from among the following persons: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Jul. 1, 2025>
  1. A public official belonging to the Senior Executive Service or a general service public official of Grade V or higher;
  2. A special service public official equivalent to Grade V or higher;
  3. A commissioned officer of field-grade rank or higher;
  4. A civilian military employee in general service of Grade V or higher or equivalent thereto;
  5. A public official in special service of class V or higher (excluding soldiers or civilian personnel in the military).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 8 (Duties of chairperson) (1) The chairperson shall represent the Personnel Management Committee and exercise overall control over the affairs of the Personnel Management Committee.
  (2) Where the chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties due to any unavoidable cause, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on behalf of the chairperson.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 8-2 (Meetings) (1) The chairperson shall convene and preside over meetings of the Personnel Management Committee.
  (2) A meeting of the Personnel Management Committee shall be convened with the attendance of a majority of the incumbent members and resolutions shall be adopted with the approval of a majority of the members present; provided, matters concerning ex officio dismissal shall be resolved with the approval of a majority of the incumbent members.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 8-3 (Secretary) (1) The Personnel Management Committee shall have one executive secretary to handle administrative affairs.
  (2) The executive secretary of the Personnel Management Committee established in the Ministry of National Defense shall be appointed by the Minister of National Defense from among public officials under his or her authority, and the executive secretary of the Personnel Management Committee established in a military unit shall be appointed by the commander of the military unit from among public officials under his or her authority.
  [This Article Added on Mar. 26, 2010]

CHAPTER III APPOINTMENT

Article 9 (Timely filling of vacancies) (1) The commander of each level of military unit shall take necessary measures to fill any vacancy without delay when a vacancy occurs in the relevant military unit.
  (2) Vacancies of civilian military employees in general service shall be filled through new employment, promotion, reassignment, transfer, or demotion. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) When requesting the filling of a vacancy, the commander of each level of military unit shall specify in detail necessary matters such as the number of personnel to be filled by occupational group and by rank and the method of filling the vacancy; provided, when filling a vacancy through a career-based competitive examination for appointment under the main sentence, other than the subparagraphs, of Article 7(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "career-based competitive examination for appointment") or an examination not targeting a large number of persons under the proviso, other than the subparagraphs, of Article 7(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "examination not targeting a large number of persons"), the commander shall specify in detail the number of personnel to be filled by occupational group and by rank, the necessity of appointment through the career-based competitive examination for appointment or the examination not targeting a large number of persons, the duties of the position to be filled, and the qualifications required. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Feb. 20, 2025>
  (4) Where a dismissal, removal from office, or ex officio dismissal under Article 28(1)2 or 5 of the Act is imposed, a successor shall not be newly employed within 60 days from the date of such disposition.
  (5) The number of persons to fill a vacancy shall be determined according to the following classifications: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. Civilian military employees in general service of Grade III or higher or equivalent thereto: by occupational group, by rank, by each armed force, and by each military unit under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense;
  2. Civilian military employees in general service of Grade IV or lower or equivalent thereto: by series, by rank, and by military unit preparing the promotion candidate list.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 9-2 (Persons subject to appointment) (1) "Civilian military employee in general service prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 6(1) of the Act means Group A professional civilian military employees, among professional civilian military employees (hereinafter referred to as "professional civilian military employees") engaged in special duties under Article 3(3) of the Act.
  (2) "Civilian military employee in general service prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 6(2) of the Act means Group B and Group C professional civilian military employees among professional civilian military employees.
  [This Article Newly Added on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 9-3 (Procedure for recommendation for appointment and delegation of appointing authority) (1) Where the Minister of National Defense recommends the appointment of a civilian military employee in general service of Grade V or higher pursuant to the main clause of Article 6(1) of the Act, such recommendation for appointment (limited to cases of new employment and promotion) shall be subject to deliberation by the Personnel Management Committee established in the Ministry of National Defense.
  (2) The President shall delegate the following authority to the Minister of National Defense pursuant to the proviso of Article 6(1) of the Act:
  1. The appointing authority, except for the authority to appoint, promote, or dismiss a civilian military employee in general service of Grade II or higher;
  2. The appointing authority for civilian military employees in general service of Grades III through V.
  [This Article Added on Jan. 7, 2020]

Article 10 (Requirements for appointment through career-based competitive examination for appointment and other examinations) (1) Persons eligible for new employment through an examination for appointment under the main clause and the proviso, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 7(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc.") shall be as follows: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Sep. 5, 2017; Dec. 19, 2017; Dec. 31, 2019; Mar. 31, 2020; Jul. 7, 2020; Nov. 30, 2021; Oct. 4, 2022; Feb. 20, 2025>
  1. A person whose reason for retirement has been verified, where a retired civilian military employee in general service is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. under Article 7(2)1 of the Act;
  2. Deleted; <Dec. 19, 2017>
  3. A person who holds a certificate or license under the National Technical Qualifications Act or any other statutes or regulations, where a person holding a certificate or license related to the duties is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment under Article 7(2)3 of the Act;
  4. A person who falls under any of the following items, where a civilian military employee in general service of Grade I is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. under Article 7(2)4 of the Act:
  a. A person who holds a doctoral degree in a field related to the duties and has at least six years of work experience;
  b. A person who has been discharged from active service as a general-grade officer;
  c. A person who has retired as a public official of Grade I or an equivalent position;
  5. A person who has at least 2 years of work experience in a field related to the duties for which appointment is intended, where a discharged military person is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. under Article 7(2)5 of the Act;
  6. A person who, as of the announcement date of the examination, has not passed 3 years since retirement and has at least 2 years of work experience or research experience corresponding to the relevant grade in a field related to the duties for which appointment is intended, where such person is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. under Article 7(2)6 of the Act;
  7. A person who, at the time of the career-based competitive examination for appointment, has been residing, or whose lineal ascendant has been residing, for at least 5 years in the area where the military unit or agency to which the person will be assigned is located, where such person is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment under Article 7(2)7 of the Act;
  8. A person who has passed at least 3 years since the establishment of family relationship registration, where a North Korean defector under Article 2(1) of the North Korean Defector Protection and Settlement Support Act is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment under Article 7(2)8 of the Act;
  9. A person who has been verified to have set an example for other military personnel during combat or operation-related training, including a military person in reserve service, where such person is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment under Article 7(2)9 of the Act;
  10. A bereaved family member (referring to the spouse, child, parent, or grandparent without an adult lineal descendant, of a deceased military person) of a military person who falls under Article 60-23(1)1 or item a of subparagraph 2 of the same paragraph of the Enforcement Decree of the Military Personnel Management Act, where such person is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment under Article 7(2)10 of the Act.
  (2) The classification of certificates or licenses by the grade of appointment for persons falling under paragraph (1)3, and the career requirements by the grade of appointment for persons falling under subparagraphs 5 and 6 of that paragraph, shall be as specified in Appendix 4. <Amended on Jul. 7, 2020>
  (3) The grade of appointment for persons falling under paragraph (1)7 shall be limited to Grade VIII or lower civilian military employees in general service or the equivalent, and the standards for special areas and environments, such as islands and remote areas, shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) The criteria for determining whether a person has engaged in the conduct referred to in paragraph (1)9 shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Added on Oct. 4, 2022>
  (5) The calculation of the required work experience as a qualification for applicants for the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. shall be based on the final examination date. In such cases, when calculating the required work experience, all or part of the period of service as a non-regular worker in a field related to the duties for the intended grade of appointment may be counted as work experience as prescribed by the Minister of National Defense. <Added on Feb. 20, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Feb. 20, 2025]

Article 10-2 (Recommendation-based appointment and probationary service of local talent) (1) A person who serves on a probationary basis pursuant to Article 7-2(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "probationary employee") shall be selected by the methods classified as follows:
  1. The appointing authority shall select a person for appointment as a civilian military employee of Grade VII in general service, from among graduates (limited to those whose date of graduation falls within the period prescribed by the Minister of National Defense) or prospective graduates of schools specified by the Minister of National Defense from among schools referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act, which offer bachelor's degree programs, and other schools established under other statutes, through the recommendation of the head of the relevant school;
  2. The appointing authority shall select a person for appointment as a civilian military employee of Grade IX in general service, from among graduates (limited to those whose date of graduation falls within the period prescribed by the Minister of National Defense) or prospective graduates of schools specified by the Minister of National Defense from among high schools established pursuant to Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act, schools referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act that offer associate degree programs, and other schools established under other statutes, through the recommendation of the head of the relevant school.
  (2) The probationary period for a probationary employee to be appointed as a civilian military employee of Grade VII in general service shall be 1 year from the date the probationary service (including education and training necessary for performing duties; hereinafter the same shall apply) begins, and the probationary period for a probationary employee to be appointed as a civilian military employee of Grade IX in general service shall be 6 months from the date the probationary service begins; provided, the appointing authority may extend the probationary period of a probationary employee by up to 6 months if there is a special reason, such as lack of sincerity in the performance of duties.
  (3) The appointing authority may require a person selected as a probationary employee pursuant to paragraph (1) to undergo education and training necessary for performing duties for a certain period in accordance with Article 59(2) and Article 60(2).
  (4) The appointing authority shall designate the military unit or agency where the probationary employee will serve on a probationary basis, taking into account the probationary employee's academic performance, major field of study, work experience, aptitude, and the number of vacancies and anticipated vacancies in the relevant military unit or agency.
  (5) The appointing authority shall guide and supervise the work performance of the probationary employee, and if the probationary employee shows poor work performance or poor performance in education and training, the appointing authority may terminate the probationary service before the end of the probationary period. In such cases, the appointing authority shall report the matter to the Minister of National Defense without delay.
  (6) The appointing authority shall report to the Minister of National Defense, by no later than 1 month before the end of the probationary period, whether to appoint the probationary employee as a civilian military employee in the expected grade, taking into consideration the employee's performance during the probationary period, the results of education and training, and the results of the evaluation of qualifications.
  (7) When the appointing authority appoints a probationary employee as a civilian military employee in the expected grade, the employee shall be exempted from the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc., notwithstanding Article 15(2).
  (8) Where the appointment of a probationary employee by the appointing authority results in the actual number of personnel exceeding the fixed number, it shall be deemed that the fixed number separately exists for such personnel until the actual number aligns with the fixed number.
  (9) A probationary employee may be paid an amount equivalent to the salary of the first step of the expected grade within the budgetary limits during the probationary period.
  (10) Matters necessary for the recommendation, selection, personnel management, payment of remuneration, and appointment procedures of probationary employees, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (9), shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 20, 2025]

Article 10-3 (Restrictions on eligibility for recruitment examinations for non-majority candidates) Eligibility for recruitment examinations for non-majority candidates for the same grade under the same requirements shall be limited to 3 times, and if a candidate fails the second examination of the recruitment examinations for non-majority candidates by scoring less than 40 percent of the full marks in at least 1 subject, the candidate shall not be allowed to retake the recruitment examinations for non-majority candidates for the same grade under the same requirements within 6 months from the date of the first examination.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 20, 2025]

Article 11 Deleted. <Dec. 30, 2014>

Article 12 (Requirements for transfer) (1) The appointing authority may transfer a civilian military employee in general service under his or her jurisdiction through a transfer examination in any of the following cases: <Amended on 2017 Dec. 19>
  1. Where the appointing authority intends to transfer a person to a position of the same grade as his or her current position, who has worked or received training for at least 6 months in a field related to the duties of the position to which he or she is to be transferred, or who has received specialized school education or holds a certificate or license under the statutes or regulations;
  2. Where it is necessary to adjust the number of personnel in the relevant grade due to the revision or abolition of the organization or the fixed number of personnel;
  3. Where an appointment by promotion is intended for a civilian military employee in general service who is serving in a position without any higher-grade position in the same series within the affiliated military unit;
  4. Where the employee is transferred to the series in which he or she previously served.
  (2) A civilian military employee in general service appointed through a career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. shall not be transferred for 5 years from the date of his or her initial appointment (excluding any period of leave of absence, period of removal from office, or period during which he or she was not engaged in duties due to demotion or disciplinary suspension); provided, this shall not apply where it is necessary to transfer the employee pursuant to subparagraph 2 of paragraph (1). <Amended on 29 Feb. 2016; Dec. 19, 2017; Feb. 20; 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 13 (Principles of conducting examinations) The employment examination for civilian military employees in general service shall be conducted by grade; provided, where the head of the examination institution (including the head of an institution delegated or entrusted by the Minister of National Defense under Article 8 of the Act to conduct the examination; hereinafter the same shall apply) deems it necessary to promptly fill vacancies, the examination may be conducted separately by the area of the expected place of service, the unit of the expected place of service, or the place of residence. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 13-2 (Expansion of employment opportunities for persons with severe disabilities) (1) The head of an institution conducting a career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. may, if deemed necessary to expand opportunities for the appointment of persons with severe disabilities as civilian military employees in general service, allow only persons with severe disabilities (referring to persons with severe disabilities under the Act on the Employment Promotion and Vocational Rehabilitation of Persons with Disabilities; hereinafter the same shall apply) to take the relevant career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. <Amended on Feb. 20, 2025>
  (2) Where an employment examination is administered under paragraph (1), the head of the institution administering the examination shall determine the types of duties subject to the examination, the types of disabilities eligible for each type of duty, and other matters necessary for administering the examination. <Amended on Feb. 20, 2025>
  [This Article Added on Mar. 31, 2020]

Article 14 (Types of examinations) (1) The employment examination for newly hiring civilian military employees in general service shall be classified into the open competitive employment examinations civilian military employees of Grade V, Grade VII, and Grade IX in general service and the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. for civilian military employees in general service by grade. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017; Feb. 20, 2025>
  (2) The promotion examination for civilian military employees in general service shall be classified into the open competitive promotion examination for civilian military employees of Grade V in general service and the general promotion examination for civilian military employees of Grade V in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) When appointing a civilian military employee in general service through a change of position, a transfer examination shall be conducted; provided, this shall not apply where the transfer examination is exempted pursuant to the proviso of Article 26(3). <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 15 (Methods and subjects of examinations) (1) The open competitive employment examination shall be conducted in the following classifications:
  1. The first examination: A written examination; provided, if the appointing authority deems it necessary, a practical examination may also be administered;
  2. The second examination: An interview examination; in such cases, no person who fails the first examination may take the second examination.
  (2) The career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. shall be conducted in the following classifications; provided, only the first examination shall be conducted for the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. for civilian military employees of Grade I in general service: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Feb. 20, 2025>
  1. First examination: Document screening;
  2. Second examination: A written examination and an interview examination; provided, if the appointing authority deems it necessary, a practical examination may also be administered.
  (3) The transfer examination and promotion examination shall be administered as written examinations.
  (4) The methods and subjects of examinations referred to in paragraphs (1) through (3), and other matters necessary for examinations shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 16 (Level of examination) (1) The levels of questions for the employment examination for civilian military employees in general service shall be as follows: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. In the case of Grade V or higher, or equivalent thereto: Ability and knowledge necessary for policy planning and management;
  2. In the case of Grade VI or Grade VII, or equivalent thereto: Ability and knowledge necessary for performing specialized duties;
  3. In the case of Grade VIII or Grade IX, or equivalent thereto: Basic ability and knowledge necessary for performing duties.
  (2) Deleted. <Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 17 (Physical examination) (1) When newly hiring a civilian military employee in general service, a physical examination shall be conducted. In such cases, a person who falls under the criteria for disqualification based on the results of the physical examination shall not be employed as a civilian military employee in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The physical examination referred to in paragraph (1) shall be governed by the Regulations on Medical Examinations for the Employment of Public Officials.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 18 (Determination of successful candidates for examinations) (1) Successful candidates for the written examination and the practical examination shall be determined as follows: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017; Mar. 31, 2020>
  1. In the open competitive employment examination, successful candidates shall be determined from among persons who have obtained at least the relevant reference scores or reference grades in the English proficiency test under Appendix 4-2 and the Korean history proficiency test under Appendix 4-3, and who have scored at least 40 percent in each subject, excluding the English and Korean history subjects, and at least 60 percent of the total score in all subjects, in the order of their total scores, within the range of 150 percent of the number of persons to be selected (where the number of persons to be selected is 3 or fewer, the range shall be the number of persons to be selected plus 2 persons; hereinafter the same shall apply);
  2. In the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc., successful candidates shall be determined from among persons who have scored at least 40 percent in each subject and at least 60 percent of the total score in all subjects, in the order of their total scores, within the range of 150 percent of the number of persons to be selected.
  3. In the cases of subparagraphs 1 and 2, where there are candidates with the same score exceeding 150 percent of the number of persons to be selected, all such candidates with the same score shall be deemed successful candidates, and in the employment examination for Grade VI or lower civilian military employees in general service or equivalent thereto in the technical field, successful candidates shall be determined from among persons who have scored at least 40 percent in each subject, in the order of their total scores;
  4. In the transfer examination, persons who have scored at least 40 percent in each subject and at least 60 percent of the total score shall be deemed successful candidates;
  5. In the general promotion examination for civilian military employees of Grade V in general service, persons who have scored at least 40 percent in each subject of the written examination shall be deemed successful candidates from among those whose combined score, calculated by giving a weight of 50 percent to the written examination score and 50 percent to the score on the promotion candidate list, is at least 60 percent, in the order of higher scores.
  (2) Where the number of successful candidates in the interview examination of the open competitive employment examination and the career-based competitive examination for appointment exceeds the number of persons to be appointed, final successful candidates shall be determined in the order of higher combined scores of the written examination and the interview examination; in such cases, if there are candidates with the same total score, the head of the examination institution shall determine the final successful candidates through deliberation by the Personnel Management Committee. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Jul. 7, 2020>
  (3) In calculating the scores under paragraphs (1) and (2), scores shall be calculated to the second decimal place.
  (4) Detailed matters necessary for the determination of successful candidates in document screening and interview examinations shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  (5) Notwithstanding subparagraph 1 of paragraph (1), where the number of applicants for the interview examination in the open competitive employment examination for Grade VI or lower is fewer than the number of persons to be selected, additional successful candidates for the written examination may be determined within 1.5 times the number of shortfall (if the number of shortfall is 3 or fewer, the number of additional successful candidates shall be the number of shortfall plus 2) and a separate interview examination may be conducted. In such cases, successful candidates for the written examination shall be determined in order from among those who have scored at least 40 percent of the full marks in each subject of the written examination, starting with the highest total score, and if there are candidates with the same score, all such candidates shall be determined as successful candidates for the written examination. <Added on Feb. 20, 2025>
  (6) Where a vacancy arises in the number of persons to be appointed due to the final successful candidate of an open competitive employment examination or a career-based competitive examination for appointment (excluding civilian military employees of Grade I in general service) declining the appointment or resigning after the appointment, the head of the institution conducting the examination may, within 6 months from the date of the announcement of the final successful candidates, determine as an additional successful candidate the person whose total score, combining the written examination score and the interview examination score, is next highest to that of the final successful candidates, from among those who did not fall under the disqualification criteria for the interview examination. <Added on Mar. 2, 2011; Feb. 29, 2016; Mar. 31, 2020; Nov. 30, 2021; Feb. 20, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 19 (Announcement of successful candidates) The announcement of successful candidates for an appointment examination shall be made within 20 days from the date of completion of the final examination; provided, this shall not apply where compelling reasons exist.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 20 (Reporting on results of conducting examinations) The head of the agency conducting the examination shall report or notify the Minister of National Defense of the results of conducting the examination, as prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 21 (Joint or entrusted conduct of employment examinations) The head of the institution administering the examination may, with the approval of the Minister of National Defense, conduct the employment examination for civilian military employees in general service jointly with the head of another institution administering the examination or entrust it to another State agency. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 22 (Frequency of conducting transfer examinations) (1) The transfer examination under Article 12(1) shall be conducted within 1 year from the date on which the reason for transfer arises.
  (2) Where a person fails to take the transfer examination conducted under paragraph (1) without a compelling reason or waives the examination, such person shall be deemed to have failed the examination.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 23 (Promotion examinations for Grade V) (1) The open competitive promotion examination for Grade V shall be conducted, if necessary, to ensure a balance in promotion opportunities among each armed force and military units under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense and to select competent civilian military employees in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The general promotion examination for Grade V shall be conducted for civilian military employees of Grade VI in general service who have served the minimum required period for promotion and whose ranking on the promotion candidate list falls within 5 times the number of vacancies intended to be filled by promotion; provided, where there is a person within such range who waives the right to take the promotion examination before the examination is conducted, a person outside the range of 5 times the number of vacancies may be substituted in the order of higher ranking. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) Matters necessary for qualifications, etc. for applying for open competitive promotion examinations for Grade V shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 24 (Qualifications for application) (1) Any person who cannot be appointed as a public official under the State Public Officials Act or other statutes or regulations shall not apply for an examination for the employment of civilian military employees in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) No restrictions shall be imposed on academic background, qualifications, or work experience for persons applying for the open competitive employment examination for civilian military employees in general service; provided, this shall not apply where the Minister of National Defense deems it necessary to impose restrictions on academic background, qualifications, or work experience. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) A person who intends to apply for an examination for the employment of civilian military employees in general service shall be at least 18 years of age in the year to which the scheduled date of the final examination of the relevant examination belongs. <Amended on May 28, 2024>
  (4) Where the head of an examination institution deems it difficult or inappropriate to apply the age requirements under paragraph (3) due to the need to promptly fill vacancies or for any other special circumstances, the head may, with the approval of the Minister of National Defense, separately determine the age requirements for employment examinations for Grade VI or lower civilian military employees in general service. <Amended on Dec. 30, 2014; Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 25 (Public announcement of employment examination) (1) Where the head of an examination institution intends to conduct an open competitive employment examination, the head shall publicly announce the following matters at least 20 days before the start date of application submission through daily newspapers, broadcasting, or other effective means; provided, where there are compelling reasons related to security, the number of personnel to be employed may not be disclosed: <Amended on Nov. 30, 2021>
  1. Matters prescribed in Article 37 of the State Public Officials Act;
  2. Subjects of the examination and the allocation ratio of scores.
  (2) Notwithstanding the main clause of the part other than the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), where urgent staffing is required due to the occurrence of a disaster or other reasons, the head of the institution conducting the examination may set a different deadline for the announcement with the approval of the Minister of National Defense. <Added on Nov. 30, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 26 (Partial exemption from examinations) (1) Where persons are employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment pursuant to Article 7(2)1, 3, 5, 6, 9, and 10 of the Act, a written examination may be exempted. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Mar. 31, 2020; Nov. 30, 2021; Feb. 20, 2025>
  (2) Where a person is employed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment under Article 7(2)7 of the Act, some of the written examination subjects may be exempted as prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017; Mar. 31, 2020; Feb. 20, 2025>
  (3) In the case of a transfer examination under Article 12(1), some of the written examination subjects may be exempted as prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense; provided, the transfer examination may be exempted in any of the following cases: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Dec. 31, 2019>
  1. Where a person is transferred to the series in which he or she previously served, or where a person who holds a certificate or license is transferred to the equivalent grade in the series related to such certificate or license;
  2. Where a civilian military employee of Grade II in general service is transferred;
  3. Deleted; <Dec. 31, 2019>
  4. Where the name of the grade is changed without any change in the duties within the same occupational group in the case of transfer under Article 12(1)2;
  5. Where the Minister of National Defense deems it necessary to exempt the transfer examination in order to reassign personnel in excess of the fixed number arising from the restructuring of military units or agencies and the adjustment of fixed personnel numbers (including reassignment within the same military unit or agency).
  (4) Where only persons with severe disabilities are allowed to take the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. under Article 13-2(1), the written examination may be exempted. <Added on Mar. 31, 2020; Feb. 20, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 27 (Registration of successful candidates in open competitive employment examination) (1) Persons who have passed an open competitive employment examination (hereinafter referred to as "candidates for employment") shall register candidates for employment, as prescribed by the head of the examination-administering institution.
  (2) Failure to register under paragraph (1) shall be deemed an intention not to accept the appointment.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 28 (Preparation of list of candidates for employment) (1) A list of candidates for employment shall be prepared by grade in the order of examination scores, and shall include the major field of study and other necessary matters.
  (2) The head of an institution administering an open competitive employment examination by delegation of the authority to conduct the examination shall prepare a list of candidates for employment within 20 days from the date of the announcement of the final successful candidates and submit it to the appointing authority.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 29 (Appointment of candidates for employment) (1) The appointing authority shall appoint candidates for employment according to the order of ranking on the list of candidates for employment.
  (2) Where a candidate for employment listed on the list of candidates for employment falls under any of the following cases, the appointing authority may defer the appointment by setting a period within the validity period of the list of candidates for employment; provided, if the reason for deferment ceases to exist during the deferment period, the candidate shall be appointed:
  1. Where the candidate continues his or her studies;
  2. Where the candidate has a disease requiring long-term medical care for at least 6 months;
  3. Where any extenuating circumstance exists that makes it impossible to perform duties other than those prescribed in subparagraphs 1 and 2.
  (3) A person who desires deferment of appointment under paragraph (2) shall apply for deferment of appointment within the period prescribed by the head of the examination institution, clearly stating the period of deferment and attaching supporting documents verifying the grounds therefor.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 30 (Transfer or demotion of candidates for employment) (1) Where the appointing authority deems it necessary, the appointing authority may, with the consent of the candidate for employment and through a transfer examination, appoint the candidate for employment to a different series.
  (2) In the case of paragraph (1), subjects of the transfer examination that are identical to the subjects of the open competitive employment examination shall be exempted, and where the reason for appointing the candidate for employment to a different series falls under paragraph (3) of Article 26, part or all of the transfer examination may be exempted.
  (3) Where it is necessary to appoint a candidate for employment within the validity period of the list of candidates for employment, the appointing authority may, with the consent of the candidate for employment, appoint the candidate to the grade immediately below the relevant grade.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 31 (Guidance and supervision of probationer civilian military employees) A person with authority over assignments shall, pursuant to Article 12(1) of the Act, constantly guide and supervise the work performance of a civilian military employee in general service who is appointed as a probationer civilian military employee (hereinafter referred to as "probationer civilian military employee"). <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 32 (Education and training of probationer civilian military employees) (1) A person with authority over assignments shall provide education and training necessary for performing duties to a probationer civilian military employee.
  (2) The education and training referred to in paragraph (1) may be provided at the education and training unit of the military.
  (3) Matters necessary for the education, training, and practical training of probationer civilian military employees of Grade V shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 33 (Exemption from probationary appointment) (1) Where a person is appointed as a civilian military employee in general service for a fixed term position pursuant to Article 45(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "civilian military employee in general service for a fixed term position"), the probationary appointment shall be exempted. <Added on Dec. 19, 2017; May 28, 2024>
  (2) Any of the following periods shall be deemed as the period of service as a probationer civilian military employee, and the probationary period may be exempted or shortened accordingly: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. The period during which the person has served as a State or local public official;
  2. The period of education and training received by a person to become a probationer civilian military employee pursuant to Article 32(1).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 34 (Dismissal of probationer civilian military employees) (1) The cases in which a probationer civilian military employee may be dismissed pursuant to Article 12(2) of the Act shall be as follows:
  1. Where the score of the education and training received during the probationary period is less than 60 percent of the full marks;
  2. Where the work performance during the probationary appointment period is extremely poor.
  (2) The criteria and methods for evaluating the work performance of a probationer civilian military employee under paragraph (1)2, and other necessary matters, shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 35 (Criteria for managing assignments) (1) A person with authority over assignments shall assign each civilian military employee in general service under his or her jurisdiction to a position in accordance with the criteria for managing assignments prescribed by the Minister of National Defense. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) A person with authority over assignments shall assign each civilian military employee in general service under his or her jurisdiction to the right position by considering the job requirements of the position and the qualifications of the civilian military employee in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) A person with authority over assignments shall classify positions by grade according to the difficulty and responsibility of duties and assign civilian military employees in general service in a manner that enables them to develop their capabilities in accordance with their career and work performance. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) A civilian military employee in general service who has received special training, such as studying abroad or participating in entrusted education in Korea, or who has undergone education and training for 6 months or longer, shall be assigned to a position related to the content of such education and training, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 36 (Reassignment) (1) The person with authority over assignments shall, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so, regularly reassign civilian military employees in general service to prevent stagnation caused by prolonged service in the same position, promote proactive performance of duties, and enhance efficiency so that they may perform their duties creatively. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The person with authority over assignments shall not reassign a civilian military employee in general service to another position within 1 year from the date of appointment to the current position, except in any of the following cases: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. Where the organization or the fixed number of personnel is changed;
  2. Where a civilian military employee in general service is promoted or demoted;
  3. Where the appointing authority intends to assign a civilian military employee in general service to a position, and such employee has received special training for at least 2 months or has work or research experience for at least 6 months related to the position to be reassigned;
  4. Where the appointing authority intends to reassign a civilian military employee in general service to a military unit or agency under a different appointing authority.
  (3) The appointing authority shall not reassign a civilian military employee in general service appointed through the career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc. to another position within 2 years from the date of assignment to the initial position; provided, this shall not apply where any of the subparagraphs of paragraph (2) applies. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017; Feb. 20, 2025>
  (4) Where the appointing authority deems it necessary to reassign a civilian military employee in general service to a military unit or agency under a different appointing authority, the appointing authority may request such reassignment through the chain of command. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (5) Notwithstanding paragraph (4), a civilian military employee in general service who is subject to the general promotion examination shall not be requested or reassigned to a military unit with a different promotion candidate list preparation unit. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 37 (Personnel exchange) The Minister of National Defense, the Chief of Staff of each military service, or the commander of a military unit directly under the Ministry of National Defense may, where it is deemed necessary for the balanced assignment and efficient utilization of personnel, formulate a personnel exchange plan and conduct personnel exchange after deliberation by the personnel committee. <Amended on Jul. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 38 (Concurrent holding of positions) (1) Where the person with authority over assignments deems that the duties are similar and that there is no hindrance to performing the assigned duties, the person may have a civilian military employee in general service concurrently hold another position. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The period of concurrent holding of another position under paragraph (1) shall not exceed 2 years; provided, where any special reason exists, it may be extended within the limit of 1 year.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 38-2 (Acting duties in cases of leave of absence) (1) Where a civilian military employee in general service falls under any of the following cases, the appointing authority or the recommending authority for appointment may instruct another civilian military employee in general service under his or her jurisdiction (including a military person responsible for related duties in consideration of the nature of the duties; hereafter the same shall apply in this Article) to act for the relevant civilian military employee in general service; provided, this shall not apply where the vacancy has been filled pursuant to Article 14(1)1 of the Act due to the leave of absence of the relevant civilian military employee in general service or where a fixed-term civilian military employee has been employed pursuant to Article 133(4):
  1. Where the civilian military employee in general service takes a leave of absence pursuant to Article 71(1) or (2) of the State Public Officials Act;
  2. Where the civilian military employee in general service takes a special leave under the main clause of Article 12(1)1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on Military Status and Service, which is applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 54(2), or under Article 54(3) of this Decree (hereinafter referred to as "medical leave");
  3. Where the civilian military employee in general service takes maternity leave under Article 12(2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on Military Status and Service as applied mutatis mutandis pursuant to Article 54(2) (hereinafter referred to as "maternity leave"), or miscarriage or stillbirth leave (hereinafter referred to as "miscarriage or stillbirth leave") under paragraph (3) of the same Article.
  (2) The appointing authority or the recommending authority for appointment may direct a civilian military employee in general service under his or her jurisdiction to act on behalf of another civilian military employee in general service who is on a business trip or dispatch in response to a disaster under Article 3(1) of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety or a calamity under Article 2(1) of the Countermeasures against Typhoons and Floods Act.
  (3) The appointing authority or the authority recommending appointment may, pursuant to the Regulations on Allowances for Public Officials, pay allowances to a civilian military employee in general service who performs duties on behalf of another person pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2), within the budgetary limits. <Amended on Jul. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jul. 30, 2024]

Article 38-3 (Training for cultivating social adaptability) Matters concerning the period and procedures for dispatching civilian military employees in general service, who have less than 1 year remaining until the mandatory retirement age pursuant to Article 14(1)4 of the Act, for training to develop their adaptability to post-retirement life shall be prescribed by the Minister of National Defense. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 27, 2014]

Article 39 (Minimum number of years required for promotion) (1) A civilian military employee in general service shall serve in the relevant grade for the period specified in each of the following subparagraphs to be eligible for promotion: <Amended on Jun. 21, 2013; Dec. 19, 2017; Dec. 31, 2019; Oct. 29, 2024>
  1. Civilian military employees in general service of Grade V or higher: At least 3 years;
  2. Civilian military employees in general service of Grade VI or higher: At least 2 years;
  3. Civilian military employees in general service of Grades 7, 8, and 9: At least 1 year.
  (2) The period referred to in paragraph (1) shall not include any period of leave of absence, removal from position, period of disciplinary action, or period of restriction on promotion under Article 40; provided, the periods of leave of absence and removal from position referred to in the following subparagraphs shall be included in the period referred to in paragraph (1): <Amended on Jun. 27, 2014; Dec. 19, 2017; Sep. 18, 2018; Dec. 31, 2019; Jul. 1, 2025>
  1. The following periods during a leave of absence under Article 71 of the State Public Officials Act:
  a. The period of leave of absence due to an occupational disease or injury under the Public Officials’ Accident Compensation Act during a leave of absence under Article 71(1)1 of the State Public Officials Act, or the period of leave of absence under subparagraphs 3 and 5 of the same paragraph or under paragraph (2)1 of the same Article;
  b. The period equivalent to 50 percent of the leave of absence period under Article 71(2)2 of the State Public Officials Act.;
  c. The period of leave under Article 71(2)4 of the State Public Officials Act (hereinafter referred to as "childcare leave");
  2. In the case of a person who has been subject to a disposition of removal from position pursuant to Article 29(1)2 of the Act, the period of removal from position shall be counted when the disciplinary action that served as the grounds for such disposition is finally determined to be null and void or revoked by a decision of the Committee for Examination of Appeals of Civilian Military Employees under Article 43 of the Act or by a court ruling; and in the case of a person who has been subject to a disposition of removal from position pursuant to subparagraph 3 of the same paragraph, the period of removal from position shall be counted when the criminal case that served as the grounds for such disposition is finally determined to be not guilty by a court ruling.
  (3) Deleted. <Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) Where a civilian military employee in general service who had been demoted or appointed to a lower grade is promoted to his or her original grade, the period served prior to such demotion or appointment to a lower grade shall be counted as the period referred to in paragraph (1). <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (5) Where a civilian military employee in general service who had retired is appointed to a grade lower than or equal to the grade held at the time of retirement, the period served prior to retirement at a grade higher than or equal to the grade at the time of reappointment shall be counted as the period of service for the grade at the time of reappointment. In such cases, the period of service prior to retirement that is counted shall be limited to the period within 10 years, calculated from the date of promotion to a grade higher than or equal to the grade at the time of reappointment for civilian military employees of Grade III or higher in general service, and from the reference date for the preparation of the promotion candidate list for promotion to a grade higher than or equal to the grade at the time of reappointment for civilian military employees of Grade IV or lower in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (6) Notwithstanding paragraph (1) and Article 41, a person who has passed an open competitive employment examination for a higher grade may be appointed by promotion when appointed to a lower grade.
  (7) Where a general service public official who has been dismissed for a reason under Article 70(1)3 of the State Public Officials Act is appointed as a civilian military employee in general service, the period of service as a general service public official at a grade equivalent to or higher than the appointed grade shall be included in the period referred to in paragraph (1). In such cases, the period of service at a grade corresponding to a grade higher than the appointed grade shall be included only in the period of service for the appointed grade. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (8) Where a civilian military employee in general service who has been demoted or reassigned to a lower grade has served in a grade equivalent to or higher than the demoted or reassigned grade, such period of service shall be included in the period of service for the demoted or reassigned grade. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (9) Where a general service public official (including fixed-term public officials and special service officials), a special service public official (excluding civilian military employees in general service other than civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position and professional civilian military employees with specialized experience), or a political service public official is appointed as a civilian military employee in general service after retirement, the period of service in a grade equivalent to or higher than the relevant grade may be included in the period under paragraph (1) as prescribed by the Minister of National Defense. <Added on Nov. 30, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 40 (Restrictions on appointment by promotion) (1) A civilian military employee in general service shall not be appointed by promotion in any of the following cases: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Sep. 18, 2018; Dec. 11, 2018; Nov. 5, 2019>
  1. Where the employee is under request for disciplinary action, request for disciplinary resolution, disciplinary action, removal from position, leave of absence, or the probationary appointment period; provided, this shall not apply where a person who has taken a leave of absence under Article 71(1)1 of the State Public Officials Act due to an occupational disease or injury under the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act is specially appointed by promotion pursuant to Article 46(1)3 or 4;
  2. Where the following periods have not elapsed since the date of completion of the execution of a disciplinary action [in cases of disciplinary action taken for any of the reasons specified in the subparagraphs of Article 78-2(1) of the State Public Officials Act, or for passive administration, drunk driving (including refusal to undergo a sobriety test), sexual violence, sexual harassment, or prostitution, the relevant period shall be extended by 6 months, respectively]:
  a. Demotion or suspension from duty: 18 months;
  b. Reduction in salary: 12 months;
  c. Reprimand: 6 months.
  (2) Where a civilian military employee in general service, who is subject to restrictions on promotion under paragraph (1), receives another disciplinary action, the period of restriction on promotion shall be calculated from the day following the expiration of the period of restriction for the previous disposition, and where a person who is under a restriction on promotion due to a disciplinary action takes a leave of absence, the remaining period of restriction resulting from the disciplinary action shall be calculated from the date of reinstatement. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) Where a civilian military employee in general service receives an order, a decoration, a commendation from the Prime Minister or higher, or at least a bronze award under the Public Officials Suggestion Regulations after receiving a disciplinary action while in the relevant grade, the period of restriction on promotion under subparagraph 2 of paragraph (1) may be reduced by half only for the most recent and the heaviest disciplinary action received. <Amended on Jan. 6, 2017; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) Deleted. <Jun. 21, 2013>
  (5) Deleted. <Jun. 21, 2013>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 41 (Appointment by promotion) (1) When appointing a civilian military employee in general service to a higher rank of Grade III or higher, such appointment shall be made from among civilian military employees in general service in the immediately lower grade within the same occupational group, and when appointing a civilian military employee in general service to a higher rank of Grade IV or lower, such appointment shall be made from among civilian military employees in general service in the immediately lower grade within the same series. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) When appointing or recommending for appointment a civilian military employee in general service to a higher rank of Grade III or higher, such appointment or recommendation shall be made in consideration of the employee's contributions to the military, capabilities, experience, major field of study, character, aptitude, and period of expected service after promotion.
  (3) When appointing or recommending for appointment a civilian military employee in general service to Grade IV, Grade V, or Grade VII or lower, the appointment or recommendation shall be made, through deliberation by the Personnel Management Committee, from among candidates within the scope prescribed in Appendix 5 of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials, in the order of higher ranking on the promotion candidate list, corresponding to the number of vacancies to be filled by promotion; provided, when appointing or recommending for appointment a civilian military employee in general service to Grade IV, the appointment or recommendation may be made from among persons listed on the promotion candidate list. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Nov. 30, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 42 (Promotion examination) When appointing or recommending for appointment a civilian military employee in general service by promotion, the appointment or recommendation shall be made through deliberation by the Personnel Management Committee of the unit designated by the Minister of National Defense; provided, this shall not apply where a civilian military employee in general service is promoted to Grade V through a promotion examination. <Amended on Aug. 17, 2010; Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 42-2 (Conduct and utilization of competency evaluation) (1) The Minister of National Defense shall establish the abilities and qualities (hereinafter referred to as "competencies") necessary for successful performance of duties by civilian military employees in general service of Grade III, and shall evaluate the competencies of civilian military employees in general service of Grade IV who seek appointment to Grade III based on such competencies.
  (2) A person to be appointed by promotion from among Grade IV civilian military employees in general service to civilian military employees of Grade III in general service shall be appointed by promotion from among those who have passed the evaluation under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as "competency evaluation").
  (3) The competency evaluation shall be conducted on a five-point scale for each competency item, and shall be graded as "Excellent," "Good," "Average," "Poor," or "Unsatisfactory" according to the score range, and the passing criteria for the competency evaluation shall be as follows:
  1. Where the average score of the person subject to evaluation is "Average" or higher (referring to an average score of at least 2.5 points);
  2. Where the average score of the person subject to evaluation is at least 2.3 points, and the person has received a score of at least 3 points in not less than 1/3 of the competency items subject to evaluation.
  (4) A person who has failed to pass the competency assessment may undergo a reassessment after supplementing the lacking competencies; in such cases, if the person fails to pass the assessment 2 times in a row, the person may undergo a reassessment only after the lapse of the period prescribed by the Minister of National Defense within the range of 1 year.
  (5) The Minister of National Defense may, if necessary, entrust the implementation of the competency assessment to the Minister of Personnel Management.
  (6) Matters necessary for the implementation and methods of the competency assessment, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Oct. 31, 2023]

Article 43 (Promotion and appointment to civilian military employee of Grade V in general service) (1) Where a civilian military employee in general service of Grade VI is to be promoted to a civilian military employee in general service of Grade V, one of the following methods shall be applied: <Amended on Jul. 1, 2025>
  1. Method of promotion through a promotion examination;
  2. Method of promotion through deliberation by the Personnel Management Committee;
  3. Method of promotion of some of the candidates for promotion through a promotion examination, and promotion of others through deliberation by the Personnel Management Committee.
  (2) Where personnel committee deliberation is required pursuant to paragraph (1)2 or 3, those to be reviewed shall be selected from among candidates falling within the scope set forth in Appendix 5 of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials, starting with those ranked highest on the list of candidates for promotion, up to the total number of vacancies including anticipated vacancies. <Added on Jul. 1, 2025>
  (3) The total number of vacancies under paragraph (2) (including those to be filled by special promotion appointments pursuant to Article 46(1)1 through 3) shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense in consideration of requests for filling vacancies submitted by the heads of military units at each level pursuant to Article 9(3). <Added on Jul. 1, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Aug. 17, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Jul. 1, 2025]

Article 43-2 (Promotion by length of service) (1) Where the fixed number of civilian military employees in general service is operated in an integrated manner pursuant to Article 8 of the Regulations on National Defense Organization and Fixed Number of Personnel, a civilian military employee in general service to be promoted by length of service shall have served in the relevant grade for the period (hereinafter referred to as "period for promotion by length of service") classified as follows: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Nov. 5, 2019>
  1. A civilian military employee of Grade VII in general service: At least 11 years;
  2. A civilian military employee of Grade VIII in general service: At least 7 years;
  3. A civilian military employee of Grade IX in general service: At least 5 years and 6 months.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), a civilian military employee in general service who has demonstrated excellent performance in promoting key national agenda or other major tasks, or has shown exemplary attitude in proactive administration, may have the period of service for promotion by length of service shortened by 1 year. <Added on Nov. 5, 2019>
  (3) The calculation of the period of service in the relevant grade under paragraph (1) shall be governed by Article 39(2) through (9). <Amended on Nov. 5, 2019; Nov. 30, 2021>
  (4) A candidate for appointment by length of service to a civilian military employee in general service of Grade VI under paragraph (1) shall be a civilian military employee in general service of Grade VII who is listed on the promotion candidate list and meets the requirements for appointment by length of service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Nov. 5, 2019>
  (5) Where the appointing authority intends to appoint a person by length of service to a civilian military employee in general service of Grade VI under paragraph (1), the number of persons to be appointed by length of service each year shall not exceed 50/100 of the total number of candidates for appointment by length of service, with any fraction of a person rounded up to the next whole number. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Nov. 5, 2019; Oct. 29, 2024>
  (6) Deleted. <Oct. 29, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 21, 2013]

Article 44 (Selection and designation of civilian military employees eligible for preferential treatment and essential working-level personnel) (1) The appointing authority may select a person who has served for at least the minimum required period for promotion in the relevant grade, is not subject to any grounds for restriction on promotion, and has demonstrated excellent performance, as a civilian military employee in general service eligible for preferential treatment in the immediately higher grade (hereinafter referred to as "civilian military employee eligible for preferential treatment"). <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The Minister of National Defense may designate any civilian military employee eligible for preferential treatment in general service of Grade VI who wishes to continue to diligently perform duties in the relevant grade, has demonstrated excellent practical competence, and is recommended by the head of the affiliated military unit as being particularly necessary for the operation of the unit as an essential working-level personnel.
  (3) Matters necessary for the selection of civilian military employees eligible for preferential treatment and the designation of essential working-level personnel under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  (4) Civilian military employees eligible for preferential treatment and essential working-level personnel under paragraphs (1) and (2) may be paid allowances in accordance with the Regulations on Allowances for Public Officials.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 45 (Preparation of promotion candidate list) (1) A promotion candidate list shall be prepared for civilian military employees in general service of Grade IV or lower who meet the qualifications required for promotion by the rank for which they are to be promoted by applying the weightings of 80 percent for performance evaluation scores, 10 percent for career evaluation scores, and 10 percent for education and training scores. <Amended on Dec. 30, 2014; Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) When conducting evaluations for the preparation of the promotion candidate list under paragraph (1), if a civilian military employee in general service holds a certificate or license related to his or her rank or has work experience in a military unit performing duties in a special area or environment, such as an island or remote area, an additional evaluation score may be awarded within the limit of 5 percent of the total evaluation score as prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Jul. 1, 2025>
  (3) The timing of preparing the promotion candidate list, the method of preparation, adjustment, and other matters necessary for the preparation of the promotion candidate list shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense, and the military unit preparing the promotion candidate list shall be designated by the Minister of National Defense. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 46 (Appointment of by special promotion) (1) Persons eligible for appointment by special promotion pursuant to Article 15(2) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Jan. 6, 2017; Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. A civilian military employee in general service of Grade IV or lower whose job performance is recognized as outstanding and who is deemed to have made significant contributions to the development of the military;
  2. A civilian military employee in general service of Grade V or lower who has received the Bronze Prize or higher in the suggestion grade pursuant to the Public Officials Suggestion Regulations;
  3. A civilian military employee in general service of Grade II or lower who retires honorably pursuant to Article 74-2 of the State Public Officials Act and is recognized to have made distinguished contributions during his or her service;
  4. A civilian military employee in general service who has died in the line of duty and is recognized to have made particularly outstanding contributions during his or her service.
  (2) When making an appointment by special promotion pursuant to paragraph (1), the relevant civilian military employee in general service shall not be subject to the restrictions on promotion under Article 40 and shall meet the requirements classified as follows: <Amended on Jun. 25, 2019; Jul. 30, 2024>
  1. In the case of subparagraphs 1 and 2 of paragraph (1): the civilian military employee in general service shall have completed the minimum required period for promotion under Article 39; provided, in the case of subparagraph 1 of paragraph (1), the minimum required period for promotion may be reduced by 1 year;
  2. In the case of subparagraph 3 of paragraph (1): the person shall not have been subject to any heavy disciplinary action during his or her period of service, nor have received any minor disciplinary action on any of the following grounds:
  a. Grounds for disciplinary action under Article 78-2(1) of the State Public Officials Act;
  b. Sex offenses under Article 2 of the Act on the Protection of Children and Youth against Sex Offenses;
  c. The offense of rape and indecent act under Chapter XVIII of Part I of the Military Criminal Act;
  d. Prostitution under Article 2(1)1 of the Act on the Punishment of Arrangement of Commercial Sex Acts;
  e. Sexual harassment under subparagraph 2 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Gender Equality;
  f. Drunk driving under Article 44(1) of the Road Traffic Act or refusal to comply with a breathalyzer test under Article 44(2) of that Act;
  g. The offense of violating the Military Secret Protection Act;
  h. Divulging military secrets under Article 80 of the Military Criminal Act.
  (3) Appointment by special promotion under paragraph (1)1 or 3 shall be approved by the Minister of National Defense.
  (4) Where a person is appointed by special promotion pursuant to paragraph (1)3, the appointment by special promotion shall be subject to deliberation on whether the person has made distinguished contributions. In such cases, matters concerning the methods and procedures for such deliberation shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense. <Amended on Jun. 25, 2019>
  (5) The appointment by special promotion pursuant to paragraph (1)1 or 2 may be made regardless of the ranking on the promotion candidate list, and a civilian military employee in general service of Grade VI may be allowed to take the general promotion examination in priority.
  (6) The appointment by special promotion pursuant to paragraph (1)3 or 4 may be made notwithstanding Articles 23, 39, and 41.
  (7) Where a person who has been appointed by special promotion pursuant to paragraph (1)3 falls under subparagraphs 1, 1-2, or 1-3 of Article 74-2(3) of the State Public Officials Act and has his or her honorary retirement allowance recovered, the appointment by special promotion shall be canceled. In such cases, the person whose appointment by special promotion is canceled shall be deemed to have retired in the rank held prior to the appointment by special promotion. <Added on Jun. 25, 2019>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 46-2 (Confirmation of grounds for ipso facto retirement) When appointing a civilian military employee in general service by promotion, the appointing authority or the recommending authority for appointment shall verify whether the employee falls under any disqualification grounds for appointment under Article 33 of the State Public Officials Act or any grounds for ipso facto retirement under Article 69 of the same Act; provided, in the case of professional civilian military employees with specialized experience and civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position, such verification shall be conducted every 5 years from the date of appointment. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 30, 2014]
  [Previous Article 46-2 moved to Article 46-3 <Dec. 30, 2014>]

Article 46-3 (Conferment of certificates of appointment) (1) The appointing authority shall present a certificate of appointment to civilian military employees in general service who are newly employed or promoted. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The certificate of appointment shall bear the seal of the appointing authority; provided, the certificate of appointment for civilian military employees in general service appointed by the President shall also bear the State Seal. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), the certificate of appointment for civilian military employees in general service of Grade V or higher, among those for whom the President has delegated the appointing authority for new employment and promotion to the Minister of National Defense pursuant to the proviso of Article 6(1) of the Act and Article 9-3(2)2 of this Decree, shall bear the seal of the President and the State Seal in place of the seal of the appointing authority. <Amended on Jul. 7, 2020; Oct. 29, 2024>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 27, 2014]
  [Moved from Article 46-2 <Dec. 30, 2014>]

Article 47 (Application mutatis mutandis) (1) With respect to the appointment and examinations of civilian military employees, except as otherwise prescribed in this Decree, the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials and the Decree on Public Officials Appointment Examinations shall apply mutatis mutandis.
  (2) With respect to the personnel records, statistics, and personnel management of civilian military employees in general service, except as otherwise prescribed in this Decree, Articles 9, 25, and 26 of the Regulations on Personnel Records, Statistics, and Handling of Personnel Affairs of Public Officials shall apply mutatis mutandis. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

CHAPTER IV SERVICE

Article 48 (Oath) A civilian military employee shall, upon assuming office, take the following oath before the head of the affiliated military unit:
  I, ( ), as a civilian military employee, pledge with pride and a sense of fulfillment to devote myself, both body and mind, to the nation and the people, and hereby make the following oath:
  1. I shall comply with statutes and regulations and obey the lawful orders of my superiors;
  1. I shall stand on the side of the people and devote myself to my duties with honesty and sincerity;
  1. I shall perform my duties with creative efforts and a proactive attitude;
  1. I shall stand at the forefront in eradicating all corrupt practices, as a practitioner of justice;
  1. I shall not divulge any confidential information that I have come to know in the course of performing my duties, both during my service and after retirement.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 49 (Mandatory service of persons who complete entrusted education and training) (1) A civilian military employee in general service who has studied abroad or received entrusted education and training for at least 6 months pursuant to Article 18 of the Act (including a civilian military employee in general service who returned during the course of such study abroad or entrusted education and training and received education and training for at least 6 months; hereinafter the same shall apply) shall serve for a period within 6 years as specified in the following subparagraphs (hereinafter referred to as "mandatory service period"); provided, where it is necessary to exempt the service obligation due to special circumstances, such obligation may be exempted following deliberation by the Personnel Management Committee under the Ministry of National Defense: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. A person who has completed study abroad shall serve for a period twice the duration of the education and training received;
  2. A person who has completed entrusted education and training conducted in Korea shall serve for a period equivalent to the duration of the education and training received;
  3. A person who has completed entrusted education and training conducted in Korea outside working hours shall serve for a period equivalent to one-half of the duration of the education and training received.
  (2) Where a civilian military employee in general service who has studied abroad or received entrusted education and training for at least 6 months falls under any of the following cases during the mandatory service period, the head of the affiliated military unit shall report such fact to the Minister of National Defense without delay: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. Where the person is first assigned to a position or his or her assignment is changed;
  2. Where the person is to be dismissed or granted a leave of absence.
  (3) Where a civilian military employee in general service who has studied abroad or received entrusted education and training does not serve in a job position related to the field of education and training, the Minister of National Defense may order the person with authority over assignments to correct such situation. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) The mandatory service period under paragraph (1) shall not include any period of leave of absence under Article 71(1)1 and 3 or paragraph (2) of the State Public Officials Act or any period of removal from position under Article 29 of the Act; provided, the period of leave of absence due to an occupational disease or injury under the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act shall be included. <Amended on Nov. 22, 2016; Sep. 18, 2018>
  (5) Matters concerning the reimbursement of expenses incurred for education and training by a person who fails to fulfill the mandatory service period under paragraph (1) shall be governed by Article 36 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on the Education and Training of Public Officials. <Amended on Feb. 3, 2016>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 50 (On-duty and emergency duty) (1) Civilian military employees shall be assigned to on-duty service during holidays or outside of working hours for the purpose of guarding against fires, thefts, or other accidents, handling documents, and maintaining work-related communication.
  (2) A civilian military employee assigned to on-duty service pursuant to paragraph (1) shall prevent all accidents and, in the event of an accident, shall promptly take necessary measures.
  (3) The Chiefs of Staff of each armed force and the commanders of military units under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense shall take necessary measures in the event of war, emergencies, natural disasters, or other comparable situations, or when conducting training in preparation for such situations.
  (4) Matters necessary for on-duty and emergency duty, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined by the Chiefs of Staff of each armed force and the commanders of military units under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 7, 2020]

Article 51 (Attire) (1) Civilian military employees in general service shall wear neat attire that maintains their dignity while on duty. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The Minister of National Defense or the Chiefs of Staff of each armed force may, in consideration of duties in wartime and peacetime, provide civilian military employees in general service with work uniforms, military uniforms, or military supplies under Article 2 of the Act on the Management of Military Supplies, and may require them to wear or use such items. <Amended on Nov. 30, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Nov. 30, 2021]

Article 52 (Identification cards) (1) An identification card for civilian military employees in general service shall be issued to each civilian military employee in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) Matters necessary for the issuance and carrying of identification cards for civilian military employees in general service shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 53 (Working hours) The Chiefs of Staff of each armed force and the commanders of military units under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense shall determine the working hours of civilian military employees in general service under their jurisdiction.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 54 (Leave) (1) Leave for civilian military employees in general service shall be classified into annual leave, official leave, special leave, and special leave upon request. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Nov. 30, 2021>
  (2) With respect to leave referred to in paragraph (1), Articles 10, 10-2, and Articles 11 through 13 (excluding the proviso of Article 12(1)1, paragraph (12) of that Article, and Article 13(3) and (5)) of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on Military Status and Service shall apply mutatis mutandis. In such cases, "military personnel" shall be construed as "civilian military employees in general service." <Amended on Nov. 30, 2021; Jul. 30, 2024; Mar. 18, 2025>
  (3) Where a civilian military employee in general service is unable to perform duties for more than 30 days in a year due to an occupational disease or injury and has obtained approval for medical care for an occupational injury or disease under Article 28 of the Enforcement Decree of the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act, the employee may, upon request, be granted leave within the limit of 180 days per year. <Added on Nov. 30, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 55 (Approval for overseas travel) (1) The Chiefs of Staff of each armed force and the commanders of military units under the direct control of the Ministry of National Defense may approve overseas travel for non-official purposes for civilian military employees in general service under their jurisdiction in any of the following cases:
  1. Where it is necessary for the employee to attend a family event of a relative residing overseas or to receive medical treatment for his or her own illness;
  2. Where the employee intends to travel overseas during leave;
  3. Where the employee intends to travel overseas during education, training, or study conducted pursuant to Article 14(1)3 and 4 of the Act;
  4. Where a civilian military employee in general service who is on a leave of absence intends to travel overseas in accordance with the purpose of such leave of absence.
  (2) The period of overseas travel under paragraph (1) 3shall be calculated based on the period computed pursuant to Article 15(2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on Military Status and Service. In such cases, "the period of entrusted education or career transition training" in the formula shall be deemed "the period of education training, or study."
  [This Article Newly Added on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 56 (Prohibition of engagement in for-profit activities and concurrent positions) (1) A civilian military employee in general service shall not engage in any for-profit activity outside official duties, and shall not concurrently hold any other position without the approval of the head of the affiliated military unit. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) With respect to the scope of for-profit activities referred to in paragraph (1), Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on Military Status and Service shall apply mutatis mutandis. In such cases, "military personnel" shall be deemed "civilian military employee in general service," "Article 30(1) of the Act" shall be deemed "paragraph (1)," and "military duties" shall be deemed "official duties." <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 57 Deleted. <Jun. 28, 2016>

CHAPTER V EFFICIENCY

SECTION 1 Education and Training

Article 58 (Education and training plans) (1) The Chiefs of Staff of each armed force or the heads of military units under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense shall establish and implement plans for the education and training of civilian military employees in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The education and training plans referred to in paragraph (1) shall include the following matters: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. Matters concerning the objectives of education and training;
  2. Basic education and training courses by grade and specialized education and training courses by field of duties to be provided at education and training units;
  3. Matters to be provided through education and training at the affiliated military units;
  4. Matters to be provided through entrusted education and training;
  5. Other matters necessary for the education and training of civilian military employees in general service, in addition to those specified in subparagraphs 1 through 4.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 59 (Education and training courses) (1) Education and training courses shall be classified into basic education and training courses by grade and specialized education and training courses by field of duties.
  (2) Basic education and training courses by grade shall be provided at each stage of appointment and promotion to enable probationary employees, candidates for employment, newly hired persons, candidates for promotion, or persons appointed by promotion to cultivate the abilities and qualities required of civilian military employees in general service of the relevant grade. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Feb. 20, 2025>
  (3) Specialized education and training courses by field of duties shall be provided to enable civilian military employees in general service to acquire specialized knowledge and skills necessary for the field of duties they are in charge of or will be in charge of.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 60 (Methods of education and training) (1) Education and training shall be conducted in the manner most effective in achieving its objectives.
  (2) Basic education and training by grade shall be conducted at the education and training unit of the military; provided, if deemed necessary, such training may be conducted by the affiliated military unit or through entrusted education and training.
  (3) Specialized education and training by field of duties shall be conducted by the affiliated military unit; provided, if it is difficult to do so, such training may be conducted at the education and training unit of the military or through entrusted education and training.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 61 (Education and training at affiliated military units) Education and training falling under any of the following subparagraphs shall be conducted by the affiliated military unit:
  1. Education and training for mental cultivation;
  2. Education and training necessary for workplace and practical adaptation for newly employed persons;
  3. Education and training necessary for performing new duties for persons whose assignments have been changed.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 62 (Entrusted education and training) Where necessary for the development of capabilities and the enhancement of academic knowledge and application skills required for the performance of duties by civilian military employees in general service, education and training may be conducted, at the employee's request, by entrusting them to domestic or foreign education and training institutions or foreign military education institutions. <Amended on Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 63 (Education and training and personnel management) (1) The appointing authority or the person with authority over assignments shall, for a newly appointed civilian military employee in general service, assign the employee to a position only after the employee has completed the basic education and training corresponding to the relevant grade and field of duties. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The person with authority over assignments shall, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so, assign a civilian military employee in general service who has received education and training to a position related to the field of such education and training. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) Where entrusted education and training is to be conducted, the person with authority over assignments may designate in advance the position to which the civilian military employee in general service will be assigned after completing the education and training, and shall, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so, assign the employee to the designated position upon completion of such education and training.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

SECTION 2 Work Performance Evaluation

Article 64 (Subjects of evaluation) (1) Civilian military employees in general service of Grade IV or lower or the equivalent shall undergo performance evaluations, and the results shall be reflected in personnel management, such as appointments by promotion. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) Performance evaluations shall assess the work performance and job competency of civilian military employees in general service; provided, performance evaluations of civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position shall assess only their work performance. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 65 (Evaluators and confirmers) Evaluators and confirmers of performance evaluations shall be designated by the Chief of Staff of each armed force, taking into account the command structure and characteristics of the military unit responsible for preparing the promotion candidate list; and in the case of military units under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense, by the commander of such units.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 66 (Types and timing of performance evaluations) (1) Performance evaluations shall be classified into regular evaluations and occasional evaluations. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) Regular evaluations shall be conducted for civilian military employees in general service of Grade IV or lower or the equivalent, based on the dates of April 30 and October 31. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) Occasional evaluations shall be conducted for civilian military employees in general service who have been evaluated pursuant to Article 67, where a reason arises to adjust the promotion candidate list under Article 45, based on the adjustment date of the promotion candidate list. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) Matters concerning the detailed timing and methods of performance evaluations, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense. <Added on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 67 (Exceptions to performance evaluations) (1) No performance evaluation shall be conducted where a civilian military employee in general service has actually worked for less than 1 month during the evaluation period due to a leave of absence, removal from position, or other reasons. <Amended on Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) Where a civilian military employee in general service is newly appointed or promoted, a performance evaluation shall be conducted on the first regular evaluation date after 2 months have passed; provided, where a demoted civilian military employee in general service is appointed by promotion, an immediate evaluation shall be conducted based on the evaluation from the grade held before demotion, and where a civilian military employee in general service has been demoted, a performance evaluation shall be conducted on the first regular evaluation date after 1 month has passed, excluding the 3 months during which the employee did not perform duties. <Amended on Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) Where a performance evaluation cannot be conducted due to parental leave, dispatch, or education and training during the evaluation period, the average of the most recent 2 performance evaluations shall be deemed the performance evaluation for the civilian military employee in general service, until the first regular evaluation is conducted after returning to duty. <Amended on Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) Where a civilian military employee in general service is reassigned to another military unit with a different promotion candidate list preparation unit under Article 45, the performance evaluation of the employee shall be transferred to the new unit without delay; provided, where a regular evaluation is scheduled within 1 month after the reassignment, the previous unit shall conduct a performance evaluation for the period prior to the reassignment and forward the results, and the new unit shall take the forwarded evaluation results into account when conducting its evaluation. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (5) Where a civilian military employee in general service has changed position, the performance evaluation conducted for the previous position shall be deemed the evaluation for the current position. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 29, 2016]

Article 68 (Adjustment of evaluations) (1) Where the evaluation results of a performance evaluator and confirmer are inconsistent with the distribution rate of evaluations, the appointing authority or the head of the military unit responsible for preparing the promotion candidate list may request a re-evaluation. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) An evaluator and confirmer who has been requested to conduct a re-evaluation under paragraph (1) shall do so within 15 days from the date of the request; provided, where the evaluator and confirmer fail to conduct the re-evaluation within the period, the Personnel Management Committee shall deliberate and adjust the evaluation, taking into consideration the following matters:
  1. The distribution rate of evaluations;
  2. The balance among military units;
  3. Other matters necessary to enhance the reliability and validity of evaluations.
  (3) Where the appointing authority or the head of the military unit responsible for preparing the promotion candidate list deems the adjusted evaluation results under paragraphs (1) and (2) to be significantly unfair, the authority may request the relevant Personnel Management Committee to conduct a re-adjustment.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 69 (Disclosure of performance evaluation results) (1) Any of the following persons shall inform the civilian military employee in general service subject to evaluation of the results of the performance evaluation upon completion thereof:
  1. Evaluator;
  2. Confirmer.
  (2) The scope and procedures for the disclosure of performance evaluation results under paragraph (1) shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 31, 2019]

Article 70 (Personnel evaluation report) A personnel evaluation report shall be prepared for civilian military employees in general service of Grade II, Grade III, or the equivalent, and the results thereof shall be reflected in personnel management, including appointments by promotion. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 71 (Delegation provisions) The forms of performance evaluation sheets and personnel evaluation reports, the distribution of evaluations, and other matters necessary for performance evaluations shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

SECTION 3 Career Evaluation

Article 72 (Subjects of evaluation) Career evaluations shall be conducted for civilian military employees in general service of Grade IV or lower, or the equivalent, who have completed the minimum number of years required for promotion under Article 39, and the results thereof shall be reflected in personnel management, including appointments by promotion. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 73 (Basis for evaluation) Career evaluations shall be conducted based on the personnel record card of the relevant civilian military employee in general service; provided, even if a matter is recorded on the personnel record card, if deemed necessary, the evaluation may be conducted after verifying such matter. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 74 (Types and timing of evaluations) (1) Career evaluations shall be classified into regular evaluations and occasional evaluations.
  (2) Regular evaluations shall be conducted for civilian military employees in general service of Grade IV or lower, or the equivalent, based on April 30 and October 31. <Amended on Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) Occasional evaluations shall be conducted when there is a reason for adjusting the promotion candidate list under Article 45, based on the date of the regular evaluation.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Feb. 29, 2016]

Article 75 (Evaluators and confirmers) The evaluator for a career evaluation shall be the personnel officer of the unit to which the person subject to evaluation belongs, and the confirmer shall be the head of the unit to which the person subject to evaluation belongs.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 76 (Types of career) (1) Career shall be classified into basic career and additional career.
  (2) The evaluation period for basic career shall be the most recent 6 years from the month of evaluation, and the evaluation period for additional career shall be the 4 years prior to the period of basic career.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 77 (Grades of career) (1) Basic and additional career shall be classified into Grade A, Grade B, and Grade C career.
  (2) Grade A career shall be as follows: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. Career as a civilian military employee in general service of the same grade or higher;
  2. Where a series has been divided, newly established, or integrated due to the revision of statutes or regulations related to appointment, career in the original series prior to the establishment or the series prior to the integration, in the same grade;
  3. In cases where a person was appointed as a regular civilian military employee after serving as a probationer, the probationary period in the same grade.
  (3) Grade B career shall be career as a civilian military employee in general service of the same occupational group and the same grade or higher, except for cases falling under subparagraph 1 of paragraph (2). <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) Grade C career shall be as follows: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. Career as a civilian military employee in general service of the same grade or higher in a different occupational group;
  2. Periods of conscription or mobilization served under the Military Service Act or other statutes to fulfill mandatory service obligations prior to appointment as a civilian military employee in general service (excluding persons who were appointed as civilian military employees in general service after completing such mandatory service and were promoted);
  3. Career as a civilian military employee in general service in the grade immediately below within the same occupational group;
  4. Career as a military personnel or public official of the same or higher equivalent grade.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 78 (Calculation of career period) (1) Periods of leave of absence, periods of removal from position, and periods during which duties were not performed due to demotion or disciplinary suspension shall not be included in the calculation of the career period; provided, periods of leave of absence that are included in the minimum number of years required for promotion pursuant to Article 39(2)1 shall be deemed as service in the grade at the time of taking such leave of absence and shall be included in the career period. <Amended on Nov. 5, 2019>
  (2) When calculating the career period, it shall be calculated in units of full months. In such cases, a period of 15 days or more shall be counted as one month, and a period of less than 15 days shall not be counted.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 79 (Reporting of evaluation results) Where the head of a military unit conducts a career evaluation, the results thereof shall be reported to the head of the unit responsible for preparing the promotion candidate list of the evaluated person within 20 days from the reference date of the evaluation.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 80 (Disclosure of evaluation results) The results of a career evaluation shall be disclosed upon request by the person evaluated.

Article 81 (Delegation provisions) The method of calculating the career evaluation score and other matters necessary for career evaluation shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

CHAPTER VI GUARANTEE OF STATUS

SECTION 1 Dismissal and Demotion

Article 82 (Dismissal due to disability) (1) Dismissal due to physical or mental disability under Article 28(1)1 of the Act shall be limited to cases where a civilian military employee in general service is unable to return to duty or is deemed unable to perform duties even after the expiration of the leave of absence period under Article 72(1)1 of the State Public Officials Act due to illness or injury. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) A civilian military employee in general service falling under paragraph (1) may submit materials favorable to himself or herself to the Personnel Management Committee or attend the committee to present his or her opinion. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) The appointing authority may utilize materials such as medical certificates or opinions from specialized institutions necessary to assess the degree of physical or mental disability of the person subject to review.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 83 (Dismissal due to poor work performance) (1) A case where work performance is extremely poor under Article 28(1)2 of the Act shall refer to any of the following cases:
  1. Where the person lacks the ability to perform the duties assigned to him or her;
  2. Where the person's performance in education and training required for duties is poor;
  3. Where the person is grossly negligent in or abandons duties;
  4. Where the person receives poor performance evaluation scores on 2 or more consecutive occasions.
  (2) Detailed criteria for cases where job performance is extremely poor under paragraph (1), and other necessary matters, shall be prescribed by the Decree of the Ministry of National Defense.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 84 (Dismissal due to revision or abolition of organization) Dismissal under Article 28(1)3 of the Act shall be permitted only when demotion, change of position, or transfer to another military unit is not possible.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 85 (Dismissal and demotion due to excess in fixed number of personnel) (1) Where dismissal is made under Article 28(1)3 of the Act or demotion is made under Article 30(1) of the Act, such decision shall be made in the following order of priority:
  1. A person under the period of suspension from duty, reduction in salary, or removal from position;
  2. A person within 7 years from the completion of the execution of a suspension from duty or within 5 years from the completion of the execution of a reduction in salary;
  3. A person with a lower ranking on the promotion candidate list;
  4. A person with a lower performance evaluation score.
  (2) The determination of a person subject to dismissal under paragraph (1) shall be limited to cases where transfer to another military unit, demotion, or change of position is not possible.
  (3) Where a determination cannot be made based on the order of priority under paragraph (1), it shall be made in accordance with the standards prescribed by the Chief of Staff of each armed force or, in the case of a military unit under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense, by the head of such unit.
  (4) Where a civilian military employee in general service is demoted, he or she shall be appointed to the immediately lower grade. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 86 (Method of promotion for demoted personnel) Where 2 or more civilian military employees in general service have been demoted to the same grade, promotion shall be made in the order of the date of demotion; provided, where the dates of demotion are the same, promotion shall be made in the order of the date of appointment to the grade held prior to demotion. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 87 (Issuance of written explanation of grounds for disposition) Where the authority to take personnel action takes a disposition to grant a leave of absence, remove from position, demote, or dismiss ex officio a civilian military employee in general service, the authority shall issue to the employee a written explanation stating the grounds for such disposition; provided, this shall not apply where the disposition to demote or to grant a leave of absence is made at the request of the employee. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 88 Deleted. <Mar. 3, 1999>

Article 88-2 Deleted. <Jul. 1, 2009>

SECTION 2 Petitions

Article 89 (Filing of petitions on personnel management) (1) When filing a petition on personnel management pursuant to Article 34 of the Act, the petitioner shall submit a written petition for review (hereinafter referred to as "written petition") stating the following matters to the Personnel Management Petition Review Committee under Article 35 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Petition Review Committee"), along with a copy of the written explanation of the grounds for disposition (limited to cases where such explanation has been received):
  1. Name, date of birth, affiliation, and rank of the petitioner at the time of disposition;
  2. The authority who took the disposition;
  3. Purpose of the petition;
  4. Grounds and method of proof for the petition.
  (2) The petitioner may appoint an attorney as a representative to file the petition on personnel management.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 90 (Period for filing petitions) (1) A petition on personnel management shall be filed within 30 days from the date the petitioner receives the written explanation of the grounds for disposition or the notification of the disposition.
  (2) Any period during which the petitioner was unable to file the petition due to reasons not attributable to the petitioner shall not be included in the period under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 91 (Composition of the Petition Review Committee) (1) The Petition Review Committee shall be composed of not less than 5 but not more than 9 members, including 1 chairperson, and the chairperson shall be the member holding the highest rank among the members falling under paragraph (2)2 through 4.
  (2) Members of the Petition Review Committee shall be appointed by the Minister of National Defense from among persons who fall under any of the following subparagraphs and have extensive knowledge of military administration; provided, persons falling under subparagraphs 2 through 4 shall hold a higher position than the petitioner:
  1. A person who has at least 5 years of experience as a judge, prosecutor, military judge advocate, or attorney-at-law;
  2. A public official belonging to the Senior Executive Service or a general service public official of Grade V or higher;
  3. A commissioned officer of field-grade rank or higher;
  4. A civilian military employee in general service of Grade V or higher.
  (3) The chairperson shall represent the Petition Review Committee, exercise overall control over its affairs, convene its meetings, and serve as the presiding officer.
  (4) Where the chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties due to any unavoidable cause, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on behalf of the chairperson.
  (5) The Petition Review Committee shall have a secretary to handle administrative affairs, and the secretary shall be appointed by the Minister of National Defense from among public officials under his or her authority.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 92 (Exclusion, challenge, and refrainment of members) (1) A member shall be excluded from the deliberation and decision on a petition on personnel management in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the member has been directly involved in the disposition related to the relevant petition on personnel management in the course of his or her duties;
  2. Where the member is a relative of the petitioner;
  3. Where the member has been designated as a witness or expert for the relevant petition on personnel management.
  (2) A petitioner may challenge a member if there is any reason to believe that the member cannot be expected to be impartial in the deliberation or decision.
  (3) The Petition Review Committee shall render a decision on a request for challenge under paragraph (2).
  (4) A member may refrain from participating in the deliberation and decision if he or she falls under any of the grounds referred to in paragraph (1) or (2).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 93 (Correction orders) (1) Where a written petition contains any defect, the Petition Review Committee shall order the correction thereof within a reasonable period, within 7 days from the date of receipt of the written petition, and if the petitioner fails to make the correction within the period, the petition shall be deemed withdrawn; provided, where the defect is minor, the Petition Review Committee may correct it ex officio.
  (2) The processing period for a petition subject to a correction order under paragraph (1) shall be calculated from the date the correction is completed.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 94 (Review of petitions) (1) Upon receipt of a written petition, the Petition Review Committee shall deliberate and render a decision within 30 days from the date of receipt, unless there is a compelling reason not to do so.
  (2) Where deemed necessary for deliberation, the Petition Review Committee may summon and question witnesses, request an explanatory statement from the person who imposed the disposition, or require the head of the affiliated military unit to submit relevant materials or documents for review.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 95 (Right to make statements) (1) The Petition Review Committee shall hear the statement of the petitioner in reviewing a case, unless it finds the petition to be well-grounded; provided, this shall not apply where the petitioner fails to appear after being notified to appear on 2 or more occasions.
  (2) A petitioner who has received a notice under Article 98(1) but is unable to attend may submit a written statement of opinion.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 96 (Designation of representatives for petitions and submission of documents of designation) (1) The person who imposed the disposition may designate a public official under his or her jurisdiction as a representative to respond to the petition.
  (2) An attorney appointed as a petition representative under Article 89(2) shall submit a power of attorney to the Petition Review Committee, and a public official designated as a representative under paragraph (1) shall submit a document of designation to the Petition Review Committee.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 97 (Withdrawal of petitions) (1) A petitioner may withdraw all or part of a petition on personnel management before the Petition Review Committee makes a decision thereon.
  (2) A petition on personnel management shall be withdrawn in writing.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 98 (Notice of review schedule) (1) The Petition Review Committee shall notify the petitioner, the person who imposed the disposition, and their representatives of the date, time, and place of review so that they may attend the review.
  (2) The head of the affiliated military unit shall grant official leave to a person who attends the review upon receiving the notice under paragraph (1).
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 99 (Decision) The decision of petition review shall be made by the attendance of at least 2/3 of all incumbent members and by the concurrent vote of a majority of those present.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 100 (Preparation of written decision) When the Petition Review Committee makes a decision on a petition case, it shall prepare a written decision on the petition review stating the following matters, and the chairperson and attending members shall sign and seal it:
  1. Information identifying the petitioner;
  2. Text of the decision;
  3. Summary of the reasons for the decision;
  4. Evaluation of evidence.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 101 (Sending written decisions) The Petition Review Committee shall send the written decision on the petition review to the petitioner and the person who imposed the disposition within 7 days from the date of the decision.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 102 (Effect of decision) Where the Petition Review Committee decides to cancel a disposition of leave of absence, removal from position, demotion, or dismissal, the person who imposed the disposition shall cancel the disposition that caused the petition within 30 days from the date of receiving the notification of the decision.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 103 (Postponement of appointment of successor) (1) Where a petition on personnel management is filed, the Petition Review Committee may, within 5 days, decide to postpone the appointment of the petitioner's successor until a final decision on the case is rendered.
  (2) Where the Petition Review Committee decides to postpone the appointment of a successor, it shall notify the appointing authority and the person with authority over assignments without delay.
  (3) The appointing authority and the person with authority over assignments who receive the notice under paragraph (2) shall postpone the appointment of the successor in accordance with the decision of the Petition Review Committee.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 104 (Application mutatis mutandis) With respect to petitions on personnel management of civilian military employees in general service, except as otherwise prescribed in this Decree, the Regulations on Petition Procedures shall apply mutatis mutandis. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

SECTION 3 Grievance Review Committee for Civilian Military Employees

Article 104-2 (Composition and operation of grievance review committees) (1) A grievance review committee for civilian military employees in general service (hereinafter referred to as the "grievance review committee") established in the Ministry of National Defense and in the headquarters of each armed force pursuant to Article 36(3) of the Act shall be composed of not less than 5 but not more than 7 members, including one chairperson.
  (2) The head of the institution where the grievance review committee is established (hereinafter referred to as "head of the established institution") shall appoint the members from among the following persons, and the chairperson of the grievance review committee shall be the member holding the highest-ranking position among them: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. A public official belonging to the Senior Executive Service or a general service public official of Grade V or higher;
  2. A special service public official equivalent to Grade V or higher;
  3. A commissioned officer of field-grade rank or higher;
  4. A civilian military employee in general service of Grade V or higher or equivalent thereto;
  5. Deleted. <Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) Where the chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties due to any unavoidable cause, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on behalf of the chairperson.
  (4) The grievance review committee shall have one executive secretary to handle its administrative affairs, and the executive secretary shall be appointed by the head of the established institution from among public officials under his or her authority.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 104-3 (Request for grievance review) (1) When a civilian military employee in general service submits a petition for grievance review, he or she shall submit a written petition for grievance review to the head of the established institution, stating the following matters: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. Address, name, and date of birth;
  2. Name of the affiliated institution, and grade or position;
  3. Purpose and reasons for the petition for grievance review.
  (2) Upon receipt of the written petition for grievance review under paragraph (1), the head of the institution shall immediately refer it to the affiliated grievance review committee for review.
  (3) The petitioner may attach materials or documents that may serve as reference for the review when submitting the written petition for grievance review.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 104-4 (Procedure for grievance review) (1) When a grievance review committee receives a written petition for grievance review, it shall make a decision on the petition within 30 days; provided, where it is deemed unavoidable, the period may be extended by 30 days with the approval of the head of the established institution. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) The grievance review committee may, if necessary, request the submission of materials necessary for review from the heads of relevant military units or agencies, may commission an inspection or appraisal, or may have an employee of the committee conduct a fact-finding investigation.
  (3) Where the grievance review committee finds the written petition for grievance review to be incomplete, it may require the petitioner to supplement it within 7 days from the date of receipt of the petition.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 104-5 (Decision on grievance review) A decision on a grievance review shall be made by the attendance of at least 2/3 of the incumbent members and the vote of a majority of those present. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 104-6 (Disposition of results of grievance review) (1) When the grievance review committee makes a decision on a grievance review petition, it shall prepare a written decision, have the chairperson and the attending members sign and seal it, and promptly forward it to the head of the established institution. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) Upon receiving the written decision under paragraph (1), the head of the established institution shall notify the petitioner of the result of the review and take necessary measures to resolve the grievance.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 104-7 (Request for reexamination) (1) A person who is dissatisfied with the decision of the grievance review committee may request a reexamination by the Petition Review Committee within 30 days from the date of receiving the result of the review. In such cases, the written request for reexamination shall be accompanied by a copy of the written decision of the grievance review committee.
  (2) The Petition Review Committee shall make a decision on the reexamination within 30 days from the date of receipt of the written request for reexamination.
  (3) No further review may be requested regarding matters for which a decision has been made by the Petition Review Committee on the request for reexamination.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

CHAPTER VII DISCIPLINARY ACTIONS

SECTION 1 Disciplinary Action

Article 105 (Decision on whether to proceed with disciplinary procedures) Where a disciplinary authority receives a notification of the commencement of an investigation pursuant to Article 83(3) of the State Public Officials Act, the disciplinary authority shall, without delay, decide whether to request a disciplinary resolution or a resolution to impose a disciplinary surcharge (hereinafter referred to as "disciplinary resolution, etc."), or whether to proceed with other disciplinary procedures or procedures for the imposition of a disciplinary surcharge (hereinafter referred to as "disciplinary action, etc."). In such cases, where it is decided not to proceed with such procedures pursuant to paragraph (2) of the same Article, the decision shall be notified in writing to the person suspected of being subject to disciplinary action, etc.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Oct. 17, 2023]

Article 106 Deleted. <Dec. 19, 1994>

Article 107 (Request for attendance and submission of evidence) Where the disciplinary committee for civilian military employees under Article 39-2 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "disciplinary committee") deems it necessary for deliberation, it may require the attendance of related persons or the submission of evidentiary materials, etc. <Amended on Oct. 17, 2023>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 108 (Right to make statements) (1) The disciplinary committee shall give the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. an opportunity to make a written or oral statement; provided, this shall not apply where the person fails to appear after being notified to do so on at least 2 occasions. <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015; Oct. 14, 2021>
  (2) The person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. may make a written or oral statement of any facts favorable to him or her, including grounds for exemption from disciplinary action, etc., or may submit evidence. <Added on Oct. 14, 2021>
  (3) The person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. may request the examination of witnesses by the disciplinary committee. <Added on Oct. 14, 2021>
  (4) Upon receiving a request under paragraph (3), the disciplinary committee shall decide whether to accept the request and notify the person subject to deliberation. <Added on Oct. 14, 2021>
  (5) Where necessary for the performance of duties, the disciplinary committee may have the secretary conduct a fact-finding investigation or may commission an inspection or appraisal by a person with specialized knowledge or experience. <Added on Oct. 14, 2021>
  (6) Where deemed necessary in relation to the fact-finding investigation by the secretary under paragraph (5), the disciplinary committee may order the attendance of the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. <Added on Oct. 14, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Oct. 14, 2021]

Article 109 (Composition of the disciplinary committee) (1) The disciplinary committee shall be comprised of not less than 5 and not more than 7 members, including 1 chairperson.
  (2) The chairperson of the disciplinary committee shall be a person in a higher position among its members, and the members shall be appointed by the head of a military unit or agency (hereinafter referred to as "disciplinary authority") in which the relevant disciplinary committee is established, from among officers, civilian military employees in general service, or public officials in general service in a higher position than those subject to deliberation on disciplinary actions, etc. <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015; Nov. 30, 2015; Dec. 19, 2017>
  (3) The disciplinary committee shall include at least 1 military judicial officer or person with sufficient legal knowledge, and at least 2 civilian military employees in general service or general service public officials; where it is not possible to constitute the committee with public officials belonging to the disciplinary authority, public officials from other military units or institutions of the State or local governments may be appointed. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (4) Where the disciplinary committee is constituted to deliberate on a case of sexual violence as defined in Article 2 of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes, or sexual harassment as defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Gender Equality, at least 1/3 of the members shall be of the same gender as the victim; provided, where it is impracticable to constitute such a committee, the disciplinary authority shall obtain prior approval in accordance with the following classifications: <Added on Jul. 28, 2020>
  1. In the following cases: The Minister of National Defense:
  a. The Ministry of National Defense and the Joint Chiefs of Staff;
  b. The Army, Navy, and Air Force Headquarters, and the Headquarters of the Marine Corps;
  c. Military units or institutions established under Article 2(3) of the Act on the Organization of the Republic of Korea Armed Forces;
  2. In the case of military units or institutions affiliated with the Army, Navy, or Air Force: The Chiefs of Staff of the Army, Navy, or Air Force;
  3. In the case of military units or institutions affiliated with the Marine Corps: The Commandant of the Marine Corps.
  (5) The chairperson shall represent the disciplinary committee, oversee its affairs, convene its meetings, and serve as the presiding officer. <Amended on Jul. 28, 2020>
  (6) Where the chairperson is unable to perform his or her duties due to any unavoidable cause, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on behalf of the chairperson. <Amended on Jul. 28, 2020>
  (7) The disciplinary committee shall have a secretary to handle its administrative affairs, and the secretary shall be appointed by the disciplinary authority from among the public officials under his or her authority. <Amended on Jul. 28, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 110 (Mutatis mutandis application of exclusion, challenge, and recusal) `Article 92 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the exclusion, challenge, and recusal of members of the disciplinary committee.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 111 (Request for deliberation on disciplinary action) (1) Where the head of a unit or institution or the head of another administrative agency who does not have the authority to request a deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. recognizes that a civilian military employee has committed an act falling under Articles 37 and 37-2 of the Act, such person shall notify the head of the unit or institution with jurisdiction over or supervisory authority over the civilian military employee, who has the authority to request a deliberation on disciplinary action, etc., by attaching related materials falling under one of the following subparagraphs as evidence of the grounds for disciplinary action, etc.: <Amended on Oct. 17, 2023>
  1. In the case of an investigation conducted by the Board of Audit and Inspection of Korea: records of investigation such as a disciplinary action, etc. request form, written responses and confirmations from the suspect or related persons;
  2. In the case of an investigation by an investigative agency: records of investigation such as a notification of disposition of a criminal case, indictment, interrogation records, and written statements of the suspect, related persons, or witnesses;
  3. In the case of a unit, institution, or administrative agency other than those referred to in subparagraphs 1 and 2: a notification of suspected facts for disciplinary action, etc. and related materials proving such suspected facts.
  (2) In any of the following cases, the disciplinary authority shall investigate whether the reported or discovered misconduct constitutes grounds for disciplinary action, etc.: <Amended on Dec. 29, 2017; Oct. 17, 2023>
  1. Where a notification is made under paragraph (1);
  2. Where the disciplinary authority discovers any misconduct committed by a civilian military employee under its jurisdiction or supervision.
  (3) After conducting an investigation under paragraph (2), if the disciplinary authority deems the misconduct to constitute grounds for disciplinary action, etc., or if a request for disciplinary action is made by the Board of Audit and Inspection of Korea under Article 32 of the Board of Audit and Inspection Act, the disciplinary authority shall request the disciplinary committee to deliberate on disciplinary action, etc. <Amended on Nov. 30, 2015; Oct. 17, 2023>
  (4) If the affiliation of the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. changes before the disciplinary committee renders a resolution on disciplinary action, etc. or a resolution on the reduction of a disciplinary surcharge under Article 112(5) or (6), the head of the former unit or institution to which the person belonged or which had supervisory authority shall transfer the alleged facts and related materials to the head of the new unit or institution to which the person currently belongs or which has supervisory authority. <Amended on Nov. 30, 2015; Jun. 22, 2021; Oct. 17, 2023>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 14, 2015]

Article 112 (Disciplinary surcharges) (1) "Property benefits prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 37-2(1)1 of the Act means the following: <Added on Jun. 22, 2021>
  1. Any property benefits including securities, accommodation vouchers, membership rights, admission tickets, discount coupons, invitation tickets, viewing tickets, and rights to use real estate;
  2. Provision of entertainment such as golf or conveniences such as transportation or lodging;
  3. Waiver of debts, employment offers, grants of interests, and other tangible or intangible economic benefits.
  (2) Upon being requested to adopt a resolution to impose a disciplinary surcharge under Article 37-2(1) of the Act, the disciplinary committee may resolve to impose such surcharge within 5 times the amount of money or property benefits acquired or provided by the acts under each subparagraph of the same paragraph (hereinafter referred to as "amount related to pecuniary misconduct," which in the case of non-monetary benefits refers to their monetary equivalent). <Amended on Jun. 22, 2021>
  (3) If, before the disciplinary committee adopts a resolution to impose a disciplinary surcharge, the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. is criminally punished under other laws or fulfills liability for compensation, etc. for the acts under the subparagraphs of Article 37-2(1) of the Act (including cases of confiscation or forfeiture), or pays an amount recovered or additionally collected under procedures provided in other statutes or regulations, then when adjusting and resolving the disciplinary surcharge under Article 37-2(2) of the Act, the total of fines, compensation amounts, confiscated or forfeited amounts, recovered amounts, or additionally collected amounts, together with the disciplinary surcharge, shall not exceed 5 times the amount related to pecuniary misconduct. <Amended on Jun. 22, 2021>
  (4) Where any of the following grounds arises, the disciplinary authority shall, within 30 days, request the disciplinary committee to adopt a resolution to reduce the disciplinary surcharge, and shall simultaneously send a copy of the request to the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc.; provided, this shall not apply where the person refuses to receive the copy: <Amended on Nov. 30, 2015; Jun. 22, 2021>
  1. Where the person against whom a disciplinary surcharge was resolved applies for a reduction within 60 days from the date on which the court's decision (including a judgment of confiscation or forfeiture) is finalized, liability for compensation is fulfilled, or recovered or additionally collected amounts are paid;
  2. Where the disciplinary authority becomes aware that a court's decision has been finalized, or liability for compensation has been fulfilled, or recovered or additionally collected amounts have been paid, with respect to the person against whom a disciplinary surcharge was resolved.
  (5) In the case of a request under paragraph (4), the disciplinary committee shall resolve to reduce the disciplinary surcharge within the limit where the total of fines, compensation amounts, confiscated or forfeited amounts, recovered or additionally collected amounts, and the disciplinary surcharge does not exceed 5 times the amount related to pecuniary misconduct, in accordance with Article 37-2(3) of the Act. In such cases, Article 40(3) of the Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the deadline for such resolution. <Amended on Nov. 30, 2015; Jun. 22, 2021>
  (6) Where the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. or the person against whom a disciplinary surcharge was resolved is sentenced to a punishment other than a fine (including cases where a fine is imposed concurrently), and it is difficult to apply paragraph (3) or (5), the disciplinary committee shall comprehensively consider the type and amount of the sentence, and whether it is an actual sentence, suspended sentence, or sentence suspended by stay of execution, and shall adjust or reduce the disciplinary surcharge accordingly. <Amended on Jun. 22, 2021>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 14, 2015]

Article 113 Deleted. <Nov. 30, 2015>

Article 114 (Resolution of the disciplinary committee) (1) The disciplinary committee shall make a resolution with the attendance of at least 2/3 of its incumbent members and with the approval of a majority of the members present. If opinions are divided and a majority cannot be reached, the most favorable opinion to the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. shall be formed by sequentially adding the next most favorable opinions to the least favorable one until a majority is achieved. <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015>
  (2) When the disciplinary committee adopts a resolution on disciplinary action, etc. or a resolution on the reduction of a disciplinary surcharge, it shall prepare a resolution document on disciplinary action, etc. or a resolution document on the reduction of a disciplinary surcharge, which shall be signed and sealed by the chairperson and the members present, and report it to the disciplinary authority. <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015; Nov. 30, 2015; Oct. 17, 2023>
  (3) The meetings of the disciplinary committee shall not be open to the public, and no person who attends a meeting of the disciplinary committee shall divulge any confidential information obtained in the course of performing their duties.
  (4) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the disciplinary committee may make a resolution in writing for matters concerning the extension of the deadline for a resolution on disciplinary action, etc. under the proviso of Article 40(3) of the Act. <Added on Jul. 28, 2020>
  (5) Matters concerning the procedures and methods for written resolutions under paragraph (4) shall be prescribed by the Minister of National Defense. <Added on Jul. 28, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Nov. 30, 2015]

Article 114-2 (Request for review or re-review) (1) Where the disciplinary authority requests a review or re-review pursuant to Article 40(5) of the Act, such authority shall submit a written request for review or re-review of the resolution on disciplinary action, etc. or the resolution on the reduction of a disciplinary surcharge, stating or attaching the following matters, along with relevant records, to the competent disciplinary committee within 15 days from the date of receiving the resolution under Article 114(2): <Amended on Jul. 28, 2020; Oct. 17, 2023>
  1. Purpose of the request for review or re-review;
  2. Reasons and methods of proof for the request for review or re-review;
  3. A copy of the resolution on disciplinary action, etc. or a copy of the resolution on the reduction of a disciplinary surcharge.
  (2) The disciplinary committee that reviews a case in which a re-review is requested against a resolution by the disciplinary committee established in the Ministry of National Defense pursuant to the main clause of Article 40(5) of the Act shall be composed of a majority of members excluding the chairperson who did not participate in the original deliberation and resolution. <Added on Jul. 28, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Dec. 30, 2015]

Article 114-3 (Use of remote video conferencing) (1) The disciplinary committee may deliberate and adopt resolutions by using remote video conferencing, in which the members and persons who are required to attend the meeting under the Act and this Decree (hereinafter referred to as "attendees"), such as the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc., witnesses, and victims, attend from separate locations equipped with simultaneous video and audio transmission devices. In such cases, the members and attendees shall be deemed to have attended the same meeting venue.
  (2) Where the disciplinary committee deliberates and adopts resolutions by remote video conferencing under paragraph (1), it shall take necessary security measures to prevent the leakage of personal information of the person subject to deliberation on disciplinary action, etc. and victims, and of the contents and results of the meeting.
  (3) Matters necessary for the operation of remote video conferencing, in addition to those provided for in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 28, 2020]

Article 114-4 (Matters to be observed by meeting attendees) (1) No person attending a meeting of the disciplinary committee shall possess any of the following items:
  1. Devices capable of recording, filming, or photographing, such as audio recorders, cameras, and mobile phones;
  2. Dangerous objects such as weapons;
  3. Other items unrelated to the deliberation of the case on disciplinary action, etc.
  (2) No person attending a meeting of the disciplinary committee shall engage in any of the following acts:
  1. Recording, filming, photographing, or broadcasting the meeting;
  2. Acts that disturb order in the meeting room;
  3. Acts that endanger the life, body, or property of others.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 28, 2020]

Article 115 (Disposition and request for approval by disciplinary authority) (1) Where the disciplinary authority receives a resolution on the reduction of a disciplinary surcharge from the disciplinary committee pursuant to Article 114(2), the disciplinary authority shall make a disposition on the reduction of the disciplinary surcharge within 15 days from the date of receipt. <Amended on Nov. 30, 2015>
  (2) Where the disciplinary authority mitigates a disciplinary action, the authority shall state the reason for mitigation on the resolution on disciplinary action and affix a confirmation signature thereon; where the disciplinary authority intends to impose a disciplinary action as resolved by the disciplinary committee, the authority shall affix a confirmation signature on the resolution. <Amended on Nov. 30, 2015>
  (3) Where the disciplinary authority is required to obtain approval from the appointing authority pursuant to Article 38(2) of the Act, despite the obligation to impose a disciplinary action within 15 days under Article 40(4) of the Act, the disciplinary authority shall request approval within 15 days from the date of affixing a confirmation signature under paragraph (2), and shall impose the disciplinary action within 15 days from the date of receiving such approval; provided, where it is unavoidably difficult to request approval within 15 days, the request shall be made within 15 days from the date on which the cause is resolved. <Amended on Nov. 30, 2015>
  (4) A disciplinary authority intending to request approval under paragraph (3) shall submit the resolution on disciplinary action and relevant documents to the appointing authority.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 14, 2015]

Article 115-2 (Mitigation of disciplinary action) (1) The disciplinary authority may mitigate the disciplinary action in accordance with the mitigation standards prescribed in Appendix 5 where the person subject to disciplinary deliberation falls under any of the following subparagraphs; provided, where the civilian military employee has previously received a disciplinary action or a warning under this Decree, any merit earned before such disciplinary or warning disposition shall be excluded from merits eligible for mitigation: <Amended on Nov. 22, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. Where the person subject to disciplinary deliberation has any of the following merits:
  a. A merit for which a decoration or medal has been awarded pursuant to the Awards and Decorations Act;
  b. A merit for which a commendation from the Prime Minister or higher has been awarded pursuant to the Regulations on Government Commendations (limited to merit awards and creative proposal awards);
  c. In the case of a civilian military employee of Grade VI or lower at the time of the misconduct, a merit for which a commendation from the Chief of Staff or the head of a central administrative agency (including agency heads of vice ministerial level) has been awarded (limited to merit awards and creative proposal awards);
  2. Where the misconduct of the person subject to disciplinary deliberation occurred due to negligence during a diligent and proactive performance of duties.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the disciplinary authority shall not mitigate the disciplinary action where the reason for disciplinary action falls under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Oct. 14, 2021>
  1. Any misconduct falling under any subparagraph of Article 37-2(1) of the Act;
  2. Failure to report or file a complaint regarding any misconduct falling under any subparagraph of Article 37-2(1) of the Act;
  3. The offense of violating the Military Secret Protection Act;
  4. Disclosure of military secrets under Article 80 of the Military Criminal Act;
  5. Crimes of rape and indecent acts under Chapter XV of Part II of the Military Criminal Act;
  6. Sexual crimes under Article 2 of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes;
  7. Prostitution under Article 2(1)1 of the Act on the Punishment of Arrangement of Commercial Sex Acts;
  8. Sexual harassment under subparagraph 2 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Gender Equality;
  9. Drunk driving under Article 44(1) of the Road Traffic Act or refusal to comply with a breathalyzer test under Article 44(2) of the same Act;
  10. Violation of obligations related to property registration or disposal and trust of stocks under Article 3 or Article 14-4 of the Public Service Ethics Act;
  11. Passive administration as defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Regulations on the Management of Proactive Administration (hereafter referred to as "passive administration" in this Article);
  12. Acts of omission or neglect of duty (excluding passive administration);
  13. Any unfair conduct under Article 13-3 of the Code of Conduct for Public Officials;
  14. Concealing sexual misconduct or any unfair conduct under Article 13-3 of the Code of Conduct for Public Officials, or failure to take necessary measures regarding such misconduct;
  15. Engaging in improper solicitation, coercion, or other unfair conduct related to the recruitment of public officials (including military personnel and civilian military employees), or engaging in misconduct related to recruitment duties;
  16. Improper solicitation falling under any subparagraph of Article 5 of the Improper Solicitation and Graft Act;
  17. Performing duties under improper solicitation in violation of Article 6 of the Improper Solicitation and Graft Act;
  18. Engaging in improper conduct using confidential or undisclosed information obtained in the course of duty (referring to information that could significantly affect judgments on the acquisition of property or economic gain and has not yet been disclosed to the general public).
  [This Article Added on Apr. 14, 2015]

Article 116 (Execution of disciplinary action) Where the disciplinary authority renders a decision on a disciplinary action, the disciplinary authority shall, without delay, issue a written disciplinary decision to the individual concerned.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Apr. 14, 2015]

Article 116-2 (Issuance of documents for imposition or mitigation of additional disciplinary surcharges) (1) Where the disciplinary authority imposes or mitigates an additional disciplinary surcharge pursuant to Article 40(4) of the Act and Article 115(1) of this Decree, the disciplinary authority shall deliver to the recipient a written disposition stating the reasons for the disposition and the amount of the surcharge or the mitigated surcharge. <Amended on Nov. 30, 2015>
  (2) If the person subject to the disposition under paragraph (1) fails to pay the additional disciplinary surcharge or the mitigated surcharge within 60 days from the date of receipt of the written disposition, the disciplinary authority may request the head of the competent tax office to collect the amount pursuant to the main clause of Article 37-2(4) of the Act. In such cases, the head of the competent tax office shall comply with the request unless there is a special reason. <Amended on Jun. 22, 2021>
  (3) Where a person subject to the imposition of an additional disciplinary surcharge has paid the surcharge and subsequently receives a written disposition for mitigation pursuant to paragraph (1), the disciplinary authority shall refund the difference.
  (4) Where a person subject to the imposition of an additional disciplinary surcharge receives a written disposition for mitigation pursuant to paragraph (1) before paying the surcharge, the person shall pay the mitigated amount.
  (5) "Size prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 37-2(4) of the Act means a division, flotilla, air wing, or an equivalent unit. <Amended on Jun. 22, 2021>
  [This Article Added on Apr. 14, 2015]

Article 116-3 (Notification of disciplinary results to victims) (1) The disciplinary authority shall inform victims of sexual violence under Article 2 of the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment of Sexual Crimes and sexual harassment under subparagraph 2 of Article 3 of the Framework Act on Gender Equality that they may be notified of the results of disciplinary action and additional disciplinary surcharge disposition (hereinafter referred to in this Article as "disciplinary action, etc.") pursuant to Article 40(6) of the Act.
  (2) A victim who has been notified of the results of disciplinary action, etc. under Article 40(6) of the Act shall not disclose the contents of such notification.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), matters necessary for the notification of the results of disciplinary action, etc. shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 22, 2021]

Article 117 (Mutatis mutandis application) Except as provided in this Decree, the Decree on Disciplinary Action against Public Officials shall apply mutatis mutandis to disciplinary action, etc. against civilian military employees. <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015; Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

SECTION 2 Appeal

Article 118 (Filing an appeal) Where a civilian military employee who has received a disciplinary action, an additional disciplinary surcharge disposition, or a mitigation disposition of an additional disciplinary surcharge intends to file an appeal pursuant to Article 42 of the Act, the employee shall submit a written appeal stating the following matters, with a copy of the written disciplinary action, a copy of the disposition imposing the additional disciplinary surcharge, or a copy of the disposition mitigating the additional disciplinary surcharge attached: <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015; Nov. 30, 2015; Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. The name, date of birth, and address of the appellant;
  2. The affiliation, rank, and title at the time of the disciplinary action, the imposition of the additional disciplinary surcharge, or the mitigation thereof;
  3. The purpose and grounds for the objection.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 119 (Composition of committee for the examination of appeals of civilian military employees) (1) The committee for the examination of appeals of civilian military employees under Article 43 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "committee for the examination of appeals") shall be composed of not less than 5 and not more than 7 members, including one chairperson; and the members shall be appointed by the head of the military unit where the committee for the examination of appeals is established (hereinafter referred to as the "appeal examination authority") from among officers, civilian military employees, or general service public officials who are in a higher rank than the appellant; provided, however, that a military judicial officer may be appointed as a member even if he or she is not in a higher rank than the appellant. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Oct. 14, 2021>
  (2) The provisions of Article 109(2), (3) and (5) through (7) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the committee for the examination of appeals. <Amended on Jul. 28, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 120 (Measures regarding appeals) (1) When the appeal examination authority receives a written appeal, it shall notify the disciplinary authority of the summary of the appeal without delay, and the disciplinary authority who receives such notification shall submit the disciplinary resolution and other relevant records concerning the appellant to the appeal examination authority without delay. <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015>
  (2) The appeal examination authority shall refer the appeal to the committee for the examination of appeals within 7 days from the date of receipt of the written appeal, unless there are special circumstances.
  (3) The committee for the examination of appeals shall complete the examination within 30 days from the date of receipt of the referral of the appeal.
  (4) When the committee for the examination of appeals completes the examination of the appeal case, it shall prepare a report on the appeal examination, which shall be signed and sealed by the chairperson and the attending members, and report it to the appeal examination authority.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 121 (Notification of decision on appeal) Upon receiving the result of the appeal examination from the committee for the examination of appeals, the appeal examination authority shall make a decision thereon within 7 days and notify the disciplinary authority and the appellant of the decision.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 122 (Implementation of decision on appeal) Where the disciplinary authority receives a notification from the appeal examination authority to cancel or mitigate the original disciplinary action, the disposition imposing the additional disciplinary surcharge, or the disposition mitigating the additional disciplinary surcharge, the disciplinary authority shall implement the decision on the appeal without delay. <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015; Nov. 30, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 123 (Prohibition of re-appeal) Once a decision has been made on an appeal, no further appeal may be filed against such decision or the same disciplinary action, disposition imposing an additional disciplinary surcharge, or disposition mitigating the additional disciplinary surcharge. <Amended on Apr. 14, 2015; Nov. 30, 2015>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 124 (Mutatis mutandis application) `Articles 93, 107, 108, 110, 114, 114-3 and 114-4 shall apply mutatis mutandis to an appeal. <Amended on Jul. 28, 2020>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

CHAPTER VIII PROFESSIONAL CIVILIAN MILITARY EMPLOYEES

Article 125 (Fields of duties) (1) The fields of duties eligible for appointment as professional civilian military employees shall be as follows:
  1. Professors at military academies and the Korea National Defense University;
  2. Instructors at military education institutions at each level;
  3. Physical education instructors;
  4. Flight instructors, test pilots, and reconnaissance and unmanned aerial vehicle pilots;
  5. Researchers;
  6. Fields that require separate personnel management due to the characteristics of duties, as prescribed by the Minister of National Defense.
  (2) Professional civilian military employees shall be classified into Group A, Group B, and Group C, according to the characteristics, difficulty, and required proficiency of duties.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 126 (Scope of application) `Articles 23, 27 through 30, 37, 39 through 42, 42-2, 43, 43-2, and Articles 44 through 46 shall not apply to professional civilian military employees. <Amended on Oct. 31, 2023; Feb. 20, 2025>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 127 (Transfer of professional civilian military employees) (1) Where it is necessary to adjust the number of personnel in a position due to a revision or abolition of the organization or fixed number of personnel, the appointing authority may transfer a professional civilian military employee to a civilian military employee in general service through a transfer examination.
  (2) Where a civilian military employee in general service meets the eligibility criteria for appointment through the career-based competitive examination for professional civilian military employees as prescribed by the Minister of National Defense under Article 128, the appointing authority may transfer the civilian military employee in general service to a professional civilian military employee without a transfer examination.
  (3) Matters necessary for the transfer under paragraph (1), including the rank and position to which the person will be appointed, shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 20, 2025]

Article 127-2 (Reassignment of professional civilian military employees) (1) The appointing authority may reassign a professional civilian military employee in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the authorized number of professional civilian military employees is transferred to another armed force, military unit, agency, or department due to organizational restructuring, revision or abolition of the table of organization or authorized personnel; and the reassignment is made without changing the job category or position group of the relevant professional civilian military employee;
  2. Where the person with authority over assignments reassigns a professional civilian military employee to another position within the same military unit or agency that belongs to the same job category, as prescribed by the Minister of National Defense.
  (2) The appointing authority may reassign a professional civilian military employee who has been appointed through a career-based competitive examination, etc. only after 5 years have passed from the date of his or her initial appointment to the position (excluding periods of leave of absence, removal from position, or suspension from duty during which the employee was not engaged in duties); provided, this shall not apply to cases falling under paragraph (1)1.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 20, 2025]

Article 127-3 (Secondment of professional civilian military employees) The appointing authority may second a professional civilian military employee to another State agency, without changing his or her job category or position group, only in cases falling under Article 41(1)1 through 4 of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials.
  [This Article Added on Feb. 20, 2025]

Article 128 (Qualifications for appointment) The qualifications and recruitment procedures for the appointment of professional civilian military employees shall be determined by the Minister of National Defense by job category and position group.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 129 (Appointment procedures) Where a professional civilian military employee is appointed, the form of the certificate of appointment and other appointment procedures shall apply mutatis mutandis to those of civilian military employees in general service. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 130 Deleted. <Dec. 19, 2017>

