CHAPTER IX APPOINTMENT OF civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position

Article 133 (Classification of civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position) (1) A civilian military employee in general service for a fixed term position shall be classified into general civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position (hereinafter referred to as "general fixed-term employees"), professional civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position (hereinafter referred to as "professional fixed-term employees"), and temporary civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position (hereinafter referred to as "temporary fixed-term employees"). <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) A general fixed-term employee means a civilian military employee in general service appointed to a position determined by the Minister of National Defense within the authorized strength of civilian military employees in general service among the following fields: <Amended on Feb. 29, 2016; Dec. 19, 2017; Dec. 31, 2019; Feb. 20, 2025>
  1. Fields requiring specialized knowledge, technology, or qualifications, such as computing, information technology, and foreign languages;
  2. Fields that can be filled only through a career-based competitive examination for appointment, etc.;
  3. Fields corresponding to the duties of professional civilian military employees, such as instructors at various educational institutions.
  (3) A professional fixed-term employee means a civilian military employee in general service appointed to perform duties in any of the following fields; in such cases, professional fixed-term employees shall be classified into Grade A, Grade B, Grade C, Grade D, and Grade E, according to the characteristics, difficulty, and required proficiency of the duties: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Dec. 31, 2019>
  1. Fields requiring professional qualifications such as attorneys, certified public accountants, or medical doctors, etc.;
  2. Fields requiring effective response for the implementation of specific projects.
  (4) A temporary fixed-term employee means a civilian military employee in general service for a fixed term position appointed for a period of up to 2 years to act on behalf of a civilian military employee in general service performing any of the duties specified in the following subparagraphs and working fewer hours than regular working hours pursuant to Article 26-2 of the State Public Officials Act (referring to the hours set by the appointing authority within the range of 15 to 35 hours per week); in such cases, temporary fixed-term employees shall be classified from Grade V to Grade IX based on the characteristics, difficulty, and required proficiency of the duties: <Added on Dec. 19, 2017; Nov. 30, 2021>
  1. A civilian military employee in general service on a leave of absence under Article 71(1) or (2) of the State Public Officials Act;
  2. A civilian military employee in general service on leave of 30 days or more under Article 54(1).
  (5) Notwithstanding paragraphs (2) through (4), a civilian military employee in general service for a fixed term position shall not be appointed to any position falling under any of the following subparagraphs as determined by the Minister of National Defense: <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  1. A position where there is a concern of involvement in vested interests;
  2. A position that requires a high level of military security and confidentiality.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 19, 2017]

Article 134 Deleted. <Dec. 19, 2017>

Article 135 (Eligibility for employment) (1) With respect to the eligibility requirements for the employment of general fixed-term civilian military employees in general service, Article 10(1) and (2) shall apply mutatis mutandis; provided, where necessary for the employment of a general fixed-term civilian military employee in general service of Grade II or higher, the requirements prescribed in the subparagraphs of Article 5(1) of the Regulations on Management of Open Positions and Publicly-Recruited Positions may apply mutatis mutandis. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017; Jul. 7, 2020>
  (2) With respect to the eligibility requirements for the employment of professional fixed-term civilian military employees and temporary fixed-term civilian military employees, Appendix 4-2 of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials shall apply mutatis mutandis. <Amended on Nov. 20, 2013; Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 136 (Employment procedures) (1) The Minister of National Defense may employ civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position under an employment contract within the budgetary limits. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  (2) When employing a civilian military employee in general service for a fixed term position, the Minister of National Defense shall publicly announce matters concerning such employment through a daily newspaper, the Official Gazette, information and communications networks, or other effective means. <Amended on Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]

Article 137 (Application mutatis mutandis of the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials) Matters concerning the period of employment, termination of employment contract, performance evaluation, education and training, personnel records, and administrative support for civilian military employees in general service for a fixed term position shall be governed by the Decree on the Appointment of Public Officials and other personnel-related regulations. <Amended on Nov. 20, 2013; Dec. 19, 2017>
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Mar. 26, 2010]
  [Title Amended on Nov. 20, 2013]

Article 138 (Handling of sensitive information and personally identifiable information) The Minister of National Defense, the Chiefs of Staff of each armed force (referring to the Commandant of the Marine Corps in the case of the Marine Corps), and the commanders of military units under the direct command of the Ministry of National Defense may, if inevitable for performing any of the following duties, handle data containing health-related information prescribed in Article 23 of the Personal Information Protection Act or resident registration numbers prescribed in subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the same Act: <Amended on Feb. 20, 2025>
  1. Duties on appointment and employment pursuant to Articles 7, 7-2, and 8 through 12, and Articles 44 and 45 of the Act;
  2. Duties on promotion pursuant to Article 15 of the Act;
  3. Duties on personnel management and retirement pursuant to Articles 21 through 35, Article 35-2, and Article 36 of the Act;
  4. Duties on disciplinary action pursuant to Articles 37, 37-2, and 38 through 43 of the Act.
  [This Article Added on Dec. 30, 2014]
  [Title Amended on Feb. 20, 2025]

